= Biology
{wiki}

<Biology> studies living systems: how <biological cells> maintain themselves, how <organisms> develop and reproduce, and how <evolution> generates diversity. Its scales connect <molecules>, <genes>, <biological cells>, <organisms> and the interactions studied by <ecology>. These are complementary viewpoints: a <protein>'s chemistry can explain a cellular mechanism, while <evolution> explains why inherited variants of that mechanism persist. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: the science of biology]{ref}.

Two organizing ideas make this immense subject navigable: shared cellular organization and descent with inherited modification. A <neuron> and a <gliding bacterium> differ dramatically in shape and behavior, yet both depend on a <cell membrane> and <metabolism>. Their differences become intelligible through specialization and <evolution>.

Keep observations separate from the <mathematical models> used to explain them. A measured <membrane potential> is evidence; an idealized <two-state ion-channel gating> equation proposes a mechanism. A <mathematical model> earns trust through predictions and comparisons with measurements. Its assumptions delimit its usefulness, and disagreement can reveal missing biology rather than a faulty measurement.

= Signal transduction
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Organism
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

An <organism> is an individual living system, made of one <biological cell> or an integrated assembly of <biological cells>. An <Escherichia coli> cell is an entire <organism>; a <neuron> is normally a specialized part of an <animal>. <Metabolism>, regulated exchange through <cell membranes>, and inherited information connect these very different scales. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology][OpenStax Biology 2e, 1.2: living systems and levels of organization]{ref}.

Keep two questions separate: how many <biological cells> make the <organism>, and how those <biological cells> are organized. A <yeast> and an <Escherichia coli> are both <unicellular organisms>, but the <yeast> is a <eukaryote> and the <Escherichia coli> a <prokaryote>. A <plant> and an <animal> are <multicellular organisms> with <eukaryotic> cellular organization. Neither distinction ranks <organisms> as better or worse: <natural selection> acts on reproduction in particular environments.

= Unicellular organism
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

A <unicellular organism> carries out its life processes within one <biological cell>. Examples include <Escherichia coli> and the <yeast> <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>. Living in a group does not by itself erase the distinction between individual <unicellular organisms> and an integrated <multicellular organism>.

= Multicellular organism
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

A <multicellular organism> consists of integrated <biological cells>, often with specialized functions. In an <animal>, a <neuron> communicates while a <red blood cell> transports <oxygen>. Cellular specialization connects <biological development> to the organization of the whole <organism>.

= Prokaryote
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

= Prokaryotic
{synonym}

A <prokaryote> is an <organism> whose <biological cells> lack a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>. The <DNA> of its <chromosomes> occupies a <nucleoid> within the <cytoplasm>. <Bacteria> and <archaea> have this cellular organization. Both contain <ribosomes> that perform <biological translation> and a selective <cell membrane>; neither is a bag of unorganized chemicals. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells][OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.2: prokaryotic organization]{ref}.

The word <prokaryotic> describes organization, not a single exclusive <clade>. Grouping <bacteria> and <archaea> by the absence of a <cell nucleus> is useful for comparing <biological cells>, but does not establish that they share a more recent ancestor with one another than either does with <eukaryotes>. See <three-domain system> and <two-domain system> for the different ancestry models.

<Prokaryotic> does not mean membrane-free. <Cyanobacteria> contain internal <thylakoid> membranes for <oxygenic photosynthesis>, despite lacking <chloroplasts> and a <cell nucleus>. Many <prokaryotes> have circular <chromosomes>, but circular shape and a single <chromosome> are not defining requirements. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-2-structure-of-prokaryotes-bacteria-and-archaea][OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.2: bacterial and archaeal structures]{ref}.

\Image[prokaryote-eukaryote-cell-organization.png]
{title=Cell organization: an <Escherichia coli> example beside a nucleated <animal> cell}
{description=Original schematic cross-sections, not micrographs and not at exact relative scale. Both show a <cell membrane>, <cytoplasm>, <DNA> and <ribosomes>. The <Escherichia coli> envelope is simplified: its outer membrane is omitted. The <animal> cell additionally shows a <nuclear envelope> and <mitochondria>; many other structures are omitted. The outlined <nucleoid> is a region, not a membrane.}

= Bacteria
{parent=Prokaryote}
{wiki}

<Bacteria> are a major evolutionary lineage of <organisms> with <prokaryotic> cellular organization. <Escherichia coli> and <cyanobacteria> illustrate its variety: the same broad cellular plan supports different kinds of <metabolism>. Most <bacteria> have a <cell wall> containing <peptidoglycan>; <archaea> do not have <peptidoglycan> walls. A <cell wall> and a <cell membrane> are distinct structures, and some <bacteria> lack a <cell wall>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-2-structure-of-prokaryotes-bacteria-and-archaea][OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.2: bacterial envelopes]{ref}.

The usefulness of recognizing <bacteria> extends beyond naming: it identifies a shared evolutionary background for comparing <genes>, <proteins> and cellular mechanisms. A familiar shape or the absence of a <cell nucleus> alone cannot distinguish <bacteria> from <archaea>; molecular comparisons, including <ribosomal RNA>, supply additional evidence.

= Escherichia coli
{c}
{parent=Bacteria}
{wiki}

= E. coli
{c}
{synonym}

<Escherichia coli>, often abbreviated <E. coli>, is a bacterial <species> widely used to study <DNA replication> and <gene expression>. Its <chromosome> is in a <nucleoid>, and its <ribosomes> work in the <cytoplasm>, outside any <cell nucleus>. A result from one laboratory strain should not automatically be generalized to every member of the <species>.

The original 1997 report by Blattner and colleagues presented the K-12 MG1655 <genome> sequence as a circular <chromosome> of $4{,}639{,}221$ <base pairs>. See \a[https://www.genome.wisc.edu/sequencing/U00096.1.htm][Blattner laboratory: original K-12 sequence and 1997 paper]{ref}. As a bookkeeping example, one complete round of <DNA replication> from one such <chromosome> produces two daughter sequence copies totaling $2\times4{,}639{,}221=9{,}278{,}442$ <base pairs>. This is twice the copied material, not twice the length of the reference <genome>; it does not specify the instantaneous <DNA> content of a rapidly growing <E. coli> cell.

= Cyanobacteria
{parent=Bacteria}
{wiki}

<Cyanobacteria> are <bacteria> that perform <oxygenic photosynthesis>: <light-dependent reactions> release <oxygen> from <water>. Their photosynthetic machinery operates on <thylakoid> membranes without a <chloroplast> or a <cell nucleus>. This is a particularly useful counterexample to equating <photosynthesis> with being a <plant>. <Chloroplasts> descend from an ancestral cyanobacterial partner through <endosymbiosis>; present-day <cyanobacteria> are independent descendants, rather than <chloroplasts> waiting to become <plants>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: chloroplasts and cyanobacteria]{ref}.

= Archaea
{parent=Prokaryote}
{wiki}

<Archaea> are a major evolutionary lineage distinct from <bacteria>. The cellular comparison here concerns the <prokaryotic> members of <archaea>; see <two-domain system> for broader <clade> usage. Their <biological cells> have a <nucleoid> rather than a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>, yet aspects of <DNA replication>, <transcription> and <biological translation> resemble those of <eukaryotes>. Their characteristic <lipids> differ chemically from the usual bacterial membrane <lipids>, and their <cell walls>, when present, lack <peptidoglycan>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-2-structure-of-prokaryotes-bacteria-and-archaea][OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.2: archaeal distinctions]{ref}.

Some <archaea> perform <methanogenesis>, producing <methane> as part of their <metabolism>. <Archaea> also inhabit ordinary soils and oceans: extreme habitats do not define the lineage. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-1-prokaryotic-diversity][OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.1: moderate and extreme environments]{ref}. Woese and Fox's 1977 analysis of <ribosomal RNA> distinguished the methanogenic group they called archaebacteria from typical <bacteria>. Their proposed three primary lines of descent were a historical interpretation, rather than the final resolution of deep <phylogenetics>. See \a[https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.74.11.5088][Woese and Fox, 1977: Phylogenetic structure of the prokaryotic domain]{ref}.

= Asgard archaea
{c}
{parent=Archaea}
{wiki}

<Asgard archaea> are an archaeal group important in studies of <eukaryotic> origins. Molecular comparisons connect their <genes> to the nuclear lineage of <eukaryotes>, rather than making modern <Asgard archaea> the direct ancestors of modern <animals> or <plants>. See the original analysis cited under <two-domain system>.

= Eukaryote
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

= Eukaryotic
{synonym}

A <eukaryote> is an <organism> belonging to the lineage characterized by a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus> and extensive cellular compartmentalization. A <nuclear envelope> surrounds the nuclear <genome>; nuclear <chromosomes> are typically linear. <Ribosomes> perform <biological translation> outside the <cell nucleus>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells][OpenStax Microbiology, 3.4: nuclear organization and protein synthesis]{ref}. Specialized <biological cells> can lose their <cell nucleus>, as mature mammalian <red blood cells> do, while remaining parts of <eukaryotic> <organisms>; see \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood][OpenStax Biology 2e, 40.2: mature red blood cells]{ref}.

<Yeasts>, <plants> and <animals> are all <eukaryotes>. Typical cells in these groups have <mitochondria>, but <chloroplasts> belong to photosynthetic <plant> and <algae> lineages, not to every <eukaryote>. A <cell wall> occurs in typical <plant> and <fungus> cells but is absent from <animal> cells. These are comparisons of representative cells, not a checklist every specialized cell must satisfy. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells][OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.3: nucleus, organelles and representative cells]{ref}.

The interesting feature is controlled separation: <cell membranes> and <organelle> membranes let different cellular processes occupy distinct compartments while <membrane transport> connects them. The <evolution> of <eukaryotes> also involved partnership: <mitochondria> descend from a bacterial <endosymbiosis>. Modern <phylogenetics> supports an archaeal ancestry for the nuclear lineage, alongside this bacterial contribution; see <two-domain system>. Thus the history of a whole <eukaryote> cannot be read as the history of just one of its <genes>.

\Image[cell-lineages-and-shared-machinery.png]
{title=Shared cellular machinery and distinguishing organization across <bacteria>, <archaea> and <eukaryotes>}
{description=Original comparison table, not a <phylogenetic tree>. The shared functions do not require identical <molecules>. Entries describe representative cellular organization and explicitly qualify exceptions; column positions imply neither ancestry nor evolutionary rank.}

= Fungus
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

A <fungus> is a <eukaryote> that obtains nutrients by absorption rather than <photosynthesis>. <Fungi> include unicellular <yeasts> and multicellular forms. Their <cell walls> differ from bacterial <peptidoglycan> walls; having a <cell wall> does not make a <fungus> a <plant>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/24-1-characteristics-of-fungi][OpenStax Biology 2e, 24.1: fungal nutrition and organization]{ref}.

= Yeast
{parent=Fungus}
{wiki}

<Yeasts> are predominantly unicellular forms of <fungi>, not a single <species> or an exclusive <clade>. A <yeast> cell has <eukaryotic> organization, including a <cell nucleus>; small size and a unicellular lifestyle do not imply membership in <bacteria>.

= Saccharomyces cerevisiae
{c}
{parent=Yeast}
{wiki}

<Saccharomyces cerevisiae> is a <yeast> used in baking and brewing and as an experimental <eukaryote>. It provides a convenient comparison with <E. coli>: both can be studied as single <biological cells>, but only <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> has a <nuclear envelope>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/24-1-characteristics-of-fungi][OpenStax Biology 2e, 24.1: yeast examples]{ref}.

= Plant
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

<Plants> are <eukaryotes>. A typical photosynthetic leaf cell has a <cell nucleus>, <mitochondria>, <chloroplasts>, and a <cell wall> outside its <cell membrane>. A root cell need not have <chloroplasts>; the <plant>'s parts specialize for different functions. <Plants> perform <cellular respiration> as well as <photosynthesis>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells][OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.3: plant cell organization]{ref}.

= Algae
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

<Algae> is a broad name for several photosynthetic <eukaryotic> groups, rather than one exclusive <clade>. The older name blue-green algae for <cyanobacteria> is misleading in a comparison of cellular organization: <cyanobacteria> are <bacteria>, with neither <chloroplasts> nor a <cell nucleus>.

= Animal
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

<Animals> are <multicellular organisms> with <eukaryotic> cellular organization. A representative <animal> cell has a <cell membrane>, <cell nucleus> and <mitochondria>, but no <cell wall> or <chloroplasts>. A <neuron> illustrates how this common plan can acquire a specialized shape and signaling function. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells][OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.3: animal cell organization]{ref}.

= Fish
{parent=Animal}
{wiki}

<Fish> are aquatic <animals>; their feeding roles depend on their diet and life stage. Small and large <fish> in the illustrative <food web> are generic groups chosen to distinguish two feeding positions, rather than a measured relationship between named <species>.

= Taxonomy
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Taxonomy
{synonym}

<Taxonomy> names and classifies <organisms>. Classification can organize known diversity and make comparisons useful, but ranks and names are not themselves evidence of ancestry. <Phylogenetics> infers that ancestry; a <clade> includes an ancestor and all its descendants. A shared feature such as lacking a <cell nucleus> need not identify an exclusive <clade>. The <organism> branch here organizes cell types for reading and is not a proposed <phylogenetic tree>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/20-1-organizing-life-on-earth][OpenStax Biology 2e, 20.1: classification and evolutionary history]{ref}.

= Domain
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Taxonomy (biology)}
{wiki}

A biological <domain (biology)> is a high-level rank used to classify <organisms> in the <three-domain system>. Distinguish a taxonomic rank from a primary branch in an inferred <phylogenetic tree>: retaining familiar names does not settle the branching relationships.

= Three-domain system
{parent=Domain (biology)}
{wiki}

The <three-domain system> recognizes <bacteria>, <archaea> and <eukaryotes> as three domains. In 1990, Woese, Kandler and Wheelis proposed the domain rank and the names Bacteria, Archaea and Eucarya, using molecular comparisons to argue that the older <prokaryote>/<eukaryote> division hid important differences. See \a[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2112744/][Woese, Kandler and Wheelis, 1990: Towards a natural system of organisms]{ref}.

The associated three-domain ancestry model places the nuclear lineage of <eukaryotes> alongside <archaea>, rather than within them. This historically influential interpretation should be distinguished from the <two-domain system>; listing three familiar groups does not establish three independent primary branches.

= Two-domain system
{parent=Domain (biology)}
{wiki}

The <two-domain system> recognizes <bacteria> and <archaea> as the two primary lineages, placing the nuclear ancestry of <eukaryotes> within <archaea>. In this model, <archaea> as a complete <clade> includes that <eukaryotic> lineage; restricting the name to <prokaryotic> members describes a different grouping. Williams and colleagues' study, published online in 2019 and in a 2020 journal issue, found support for this model and a close relationship to <Asgard archaea>. See \a[https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-019-1040-x][Williams et al., 2020: Phylogenomics provides robust support for a two-domains tree of life]{ref}.

This updates the interpretation of the 1977 and 1990 classifications rather than undoing their discovery of deep differences between <bacteria> and <archaea>. Deep <phylogenetic trees> depend on the sampled <genes>, <organisms> and inference model; the precise closest archaeal relatives and early sequence of events require further evidence. Bacterial ancestry of <mitochondria> through <endosymbiosis> is a separate part of <eukaryotic> history.

= Species
{parent=Taxonomy (biology)}
{wiki}

A <species> is a named unit of biological classification. The useful criteria vary among groups: reproductive compatibility can inform classification in many <animals>, while microbial comparisons also use <genomes> and other characters. <Escherichia coli> is a <species>; K-12 MG1655 identifies a particular strain within it. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/18-2-formation-of-new-species][OpenStax Biology 2e, 18.2: species concepts]{ref}.

= Developmental biology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Biological development
{parent=Developmental biology}

= Paleontology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Fossil
{parent=Paleontology}
{wiki}

= Ecology
{parent=Biology}

The goal of <ecology> is to explain where <organisms> live, how many there are, and how their interactions with one another and their surroundings change those patterns. Living partners include <prey>, <predators> and <parasites>; nonliving conditions include <light>, <temperature> and <water>. An <ecosystem> brings these interacting parts into one study system. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 44.1: the scope of ecology]{ref}.

Start with an invented aquatic <food web>: photosynthetic <algae> feed <zooplankton>, which feed small <fish>, which feed large <fish>. That <food chain> has four feeding positions and three feeding arrows. Dead material also reaches <decomposers> through <detritus>, so the <food web> branches rather than forming a single ladder.

\Image[illustrative-food-web.png]
{title=An illustrative aquatic <food web>: feeding arrows point from resource to <consumer>}
{description=Original schematic, not a measured <ecosystem>. Solid arrows mean RESOURCE → CONSUMER; dashed arrows mean transfer of dead material to <detritus>. Box sizes and arrow widths do not represent abundance or transfer rates. <Algae>, <zooplankton>, small <fish> and large <fish> are generic groups, not identified <species>. Selected dead-material transfers are shown; further feeding and recycling routes are omitted.}

The appeal of <ecology> is that an <organism>'s surroundings include other <organisms> that change those surroundings. <Photosynthesis> links <light> to living material; <predation> transfers that material between <organisms>; <decomposers> return its constituent <chemical elements> to forms that can be used again. These connections help explain why changing one part of an <ecosystem> can affect distant parts of its <food web>, although an arrow alone does not predict the size or even the direction of an indirect change. <Population dynamics> supplies quantitative tools when the question concerns changing counts.

In 1866, Ernst Haeckel's \i[Generelle Morphologie der Organismen] included a section titled \i[Oecologie und Chorologie] in volume 2, beginning on page 286. This places the terminology of <ecology> in an early systematic treatment of <organisms> and their relationships. See \a[https://books.google.com/books?id=tKFbIVgJS8wC][Haeckel, Generelle Morphologie der Organismen, volume 2 (1866), contents and p. 286]{ref}. A later quantitative landmark is Raymond L. Lindeman's 1942 treatment of <ecosystems> through feeding relationships and <energy flow>. See \a[https://doi.org/10.2307/1930126][Lindeman, The Trophic-Dynamic Aspect of Ecology, Ecology 23 (1942), 399–417]{ref}.

= Ecosystem
{parent=Ecology}
{wiki}

An <ecosystem> is a system of interacting <organisms> and their nonliving surroundings. A pond study might include <algae>, <fish>, <bacteria>, <water>, dissolved <chemical compounds> and incoming <light>. The useful step is to state the boundary and time interval: the pond surface, bottom and shoreline might enclose the study region, but <water>, <organisms> and <energy> can cross them. The chosen boundary specifies what counts as internal storage, input or output; it does not make the <ecosystem> sealed. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: ecosystems and their components]{ref}.

For a toy pond <water> budget over one day, begin with $1000\,\mathrm{m^3}$, add $120\,\mathrm{m^3}$ crossing inward, and remove $90\,\mathrm{m^3}$ crossing outward. With all other transfers omitted by assumption, the final amount is $1000+120-90=1030\,\mathrm{m^3}$. The same bookkeeping asks whether a change inside an <ecosystem> came from internal processes or exchanges with its surroundings. Changing the boundary to include an upstream reservoir changes which transfers are internal, while preserving the accounting.

= Primary producer
{parent=Ecosystem}

A <primary producer> builds organic <chemical compounds> from inorganic sources. Photosynthetic <algae>, <plants> and <cyanobacteria> use <light> <energy> for this role through <photosynthesis> and <carbon fixation>. The role supplies new living material to a <food web>; it does not mean that the <organism> creates <energy>.

= Consumer
{disambiguate=food chain}
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

= Consumer
{synonym}

A <consumer> obtains organic material by feeding on other <organisms> or their products. A <consumer> feeding on <primary producers> is a primary <consumer>; one feeding on primary <consumers> is a secondary <consumer>. These names describe positions in a <food chain>, rather than permanent labels for a <species>.

= Decomposer
{parent=Consumer (food chain)}
{wiki}

A <decomposer> breaks down dead organic material and absorbs products of that breakdown; many <bacteria> and <fungi> perform this role. <Decomposers> connect <detritus> from different <trophic levels> to material recycling, so placing all <decomposers> at one final step of every <food chain> is misleading. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: grazing and detrital food webs]{ref}.

= Omnivore
{parent=Consumer (food chain)}
{wiki}

An <omnivore> feeds on both <primary producers> and <consumers>. Its mixed diet explains why a <trophic level> need not be a whole number or a fixed label throughout an <organism>'s life.

= Detritus
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

<Detritus> is nonliving organic material, including dead remains and organic wastes. It is a resource for <decomposers>, not itself an <organism> or a <predator>. In the illustrative <food web>, the dashed arrow from <algae> to <detritus> denotes a material transfer; the solid arrow from <detritus> to <decomposers> denotes resource use.

= Trophic level
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{TL}$}

A <trophic level> describes feeding position relative to <primary producers>. Assign <primary producers> level 1; in the illustrated <food chain>, <zooplankton> occupy level 2, small <fish> level 3, and large <fish> level 4. Following a single feeding path adds one level at each step. The labels make dependence on the initial resource visible, but a branching <food web> can give an <organism> several feeding paths. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: feeding positions and branching food webs]{ref}.

An <omnivore> can obtain food from more than one <trophic level>. As an explicitly assumed diet-weighted example, suppose an <omnivore> gets half its assimilated food <energy> from level-1 <primary producers> and half from level-2 <consumers>. Define its <trophic level> as one plus the weighted <mean> level of its food: $1+(1/2)1+(1/2)2=2.5$. This computed position depends on the specified diet and weighting convention; it is not an immutable property of the <species>. A different life stage or diet can change it.

= Energy flow
{disambiguate=ecology}
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

= Energy flow
{synonym}

<Energy flow> tracks <energy> entering an <ecosystem>, passing through <organisms> and leaving its boundary. To see its quantitative effect, assume net production at one <trophic level> is $P_1=1000\,\mathrm{kJ\,m^{-2}\,day^{-1}}$ and assume a transfer efficiency $\varepsilon=0.10$ at each of two successive steps. Here $P_i$ measures the <energy> incorporated into new living material per unit <area> per day, after that level's own <cellular respiration>; it is not a count of <organisms> or a standing stock. The <joule> supplies the <energy> unit, with $1\,\mathrm{kJ}=1000\,\mathrm J$.
$$
P_{i+1}=\varepsilon P_i,\qquad P_2=100,\qquad P_3=10\quad\mathrm{kJ\,m^{-2}\,day^{-1}}.
$$

\Image[trophic-energy-budget.png]
{title=An assumed <energy flow> budget: 1000 → 100 → 10 kJ per square metre per day}
{description=Original toy calculation with 10 percent transfer efficiency assumed at both steps. Bars use one linear scale; the labels retain visibility of the smallest bar. The differences 900 and 90 are production not transferred along the selected <food chain>, not measurements of <heat> alone. The example is separate from the four-position illustrative <food web>.}

Only $10/1000=0.01$, or one percent, of the initial production reaches level 3 in this toy <food chain>. The first step leaves $1000-100=900$ and the second $100-10=90$ outside the next level's new production. These differences can include uneaten material, wastes, and <energy> released as <heat> during <cellular respiration>; their separate amounts have not been assigned. <Detritus> can still carry <energy> to <decomposers>. The 10 percent efficiency is an assumption, not a universal law: transfer efficiencies vary with the <organisms>, food and conditions. The production ratio $P_{i+1}/P_i$ also differs from the fraction of an individual meal converted into growth. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-2-energy-flow-through-ecosystems][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.2: production and transfer efficiency]{ref}.

The shrinking budget explains why supporting another <trophic level> requires enough production below it. It does not prove an exact maximum length for every <food chain>, particularly when a <food web> imports resources or has branching diets. Lindeman's 1942 paper grouped feeding relationships into <trophic levels>, compared transfers and <cellular respiration>, and emphasized the <ecosystem> as a unit connecting living and nonliving components. That viewpoint made different <organisms> comparable through a common <energy> budget. See \a[https://doi.org/10.2307/1930126][Lindeman, The Trophic-Dynamic Aspect of Ecology (1942)]{ref}.

Material recycling and <energy flow> describe different bookkeeping. <Carbon>, <nitrogen> and <phosphorus> can be reused through <chemical reactions> and transfers between <organisms> and their surroundings. <Energy> transferred as <heat> is conserved, but is no longer the same store of chemical <energy> available to a <consumer>; photosynthetic <primary producers> need continuing <light> input. A <decomposer> helps recycle material and also releases <heat> through <metabolism>. Thus loss from one feeding budget means transfer into other forms or routes, never destruction of <energy>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-3-biogeochemical-cycles][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.3: material cycles]{ref}. <Conservation of energy> applies to the whole accounting.

= Zooplankton
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

<Zooplankton> are drifting aquatic <consumers>, including small <animals>. In the illustrative <food web>, the chosen <zooplankton> feed on <algae> and serve as <prey> for small <fish>; this assumed diet is not a claim about every kind of <zooplankton>.

= Endosymbiosis
{parent=Ecology}

<Endosymbiosis> is an association in which one <organism> lives within another. The evolutionary incorporation of bacterial partners gave rise to <mitochondria> and, in photosynthetic lineages, <chloroplasts>. Their retained <DNA> and <ribosomes>, and molecular relationships to <bacteria>, provide evidence beyond visual resemblance. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins][OpenStax Biology 2e, 23.1: endosymbiotic origins]{ref}.

= Parasitism
{parent=Ecology}

<Parasitism> is an interaction in which a <parasite> obtains resources from a <host>, reducing some component of the <host>'s performance. Its demographic effects depend on exposure, within-host processes and transmission opportunities.

= Parasitoid
{parent=Parasitism}

A <parasitoid> develops on or in a <host> and eventually kills it. Host-parasitoid transmission therefore has different offspring accounting from ordinary <predation> or a nonlethal parasite-burden model.

= Host
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Parasitism}

= Host
{synonym}

A biological <host> provides the environment or resources used by a <parasite> or other associated organism.

= Parasite
{parent=Parasitism}

A <parasite> is an organism benefiting from <parasitism>. <Microparasite> and <macroparasite> distinguish useful transmission-model descriptions of within-host multiplication and distributed <parasite> burden.

= Macroparasite
{parent=Parasite}

A <macroparasite> is typically modelled using the number of <parasites> carried by each <host>. Adult parasitic worms illustrate why <within-host parasite burden>, developmental stages, mating and <host> exposure heterogeneity matter beyond an infected/not-infected classification.

= Within-host parasite burden
{parent=Macroparasite}
{title2=$j$}

The <within-host parasite burden> is the number of <parasites>, or a specified parasite-load measure, carried by one <host>. A population's <mean> burden and its infected-host <prevalence> are different summaries of the burden distribution.

= Microparasite
{parent=Parasite}

A <microparasite> is typically modelled as multiplying rapidly within a <host>, so infection status rather than the exact organism count is the first between-host state variable. <SIS model>, <SIR model> and <SEIR model> descriptions are examples. Additional within-host structure can still be required.

= Predation
{parent=Ecology}

<Predation> is the consumption of <prey> by a <predator>, affecting the demographic rates of both populations.

In the usual animal-feeding sense, a <predator> kills and eats its <prey>. This distinguishes the interaction from feeding on already-dead material and from the prolonged exploitation of a living <host> in <parasitism>. In the illustrative <food web>, a small <fish> eating <zooplankton> is a <predator>; a large <fish> eating that small <fish> makes the same small <fish> its <prey>. These roles belong to a particular interaction. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/45-6-community-ecology][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 45.6: predation and community interactions]{ref}.

For a toy count, suppose 50 small <fish> begin a day and 8 are eaten by large <fish>. With births, other deaths and movement explicitly omitted, 42 small <fish> remain, and the fraction removed by <predation> is $8/50=0.16$. This measures removal of <prey>, not production of new <predators>. Food intake can support survival and reproduction, but eight meals do not imply eight new <predators>; <energy flow> and life-history details intervene. <Lotka-Volterra equations> provide one idealized account of such <population dynamics>, rather than a guarantee that every interacting pair must cycle.

= Prey
{parent=Predation}

<Prey> is an <organism> consumed by a <predator> in <predation>. The name describes the receiving end of that interaction: a small <fish> can be <prey> for a large <fish> while being a <predator> of <zooplankton>. In a <food web> drawn with resource-to-<consumer> arrows, a feeding arrow goes outward from the <prey> toward its <predator>. This convention makes the route of food and <energy> easy to follow.

= Predator
{parent=Predation}

A <predator> is an <organism> that consumes <prey> through <predation>. A <predator> need not occupy the highest <trophic level> of an <ecosystem>, and the label does not specify its size or guarantee that it has no <predators> of its own. In the illustrative <food chain>, the small <fish> is a <predator> at level 3 and also <prey> for the large <fish> at level 4. Separating these relational labels from <species> names prevents a misleading division of the world into permanent <predator> and <prey> categories.

= Protein
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <protein> is a biological <molecule> made of one or more <polypeptide chains>: ordered <amino acid residues> joined by <peptide bonds>. <Biological translation> assembles a chain in the order specified by <messenger RNA> <codons>. <Protein folding> then brings parts of that chain into spatial relationships that support its function. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/][Cooper, The Cell: protein composition and structure]{ref}. These processes can overlap in a <biological cell>; the sequence-to-shape explanation does not require waiting until <biological translation> finishes before any folding begins. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/][Cooper, The Cell: protein folding and processing]{ref}.

The useful connection is from information to physical work. A <protein>'s <protein primary structure> records an order; its <protein tertiary structure> can bring distant <amino acid residues> together at an <active site>. An <enzyme> uses such an arrangement to recognize a <substrate> and accelerate a <chemical reaction>. Other <proteins> serve as <receptors>, <membrane transport proteins> or structural material. For example, <actin> assembles into filaments whose mechanical properties can be described by <biopolymer mechanics>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: protein function and binding]{ref}.

Order matters beyond composition. Let A stand for <alanine> and M for <methionine>, and read from the <N-terminus> to the <C-terminus>. The illustrative strings AMAMAMAMAMAM and AAAAAAMMMMMM each contain exactly six A and six M <amino acid residues>. Each has $12-1=11$ <peptide bonds> when made into one unbranched <polypeptide chain>. In the first string all eleven neighboring pairs have different letters; in the second only the pair at positions 6 and 7 does. A composition <histogram> therefore loses information about which <amino acid residues> are neighbors. Their arrangements can affect <protein folding> and function; these short invented strings neither specify a known functional <protein> nor establish what shape either would adopt.

\Image[protein-sequence-to-fold.png]
{title=From <amino acid> order to an imagined fold and binding site}
{description=Original schematic using two twelve-residue strings with equal composition. The imagined fold uses the first string: colors and numbers preserve its <protein primary structure>, while positions 4 and 9 approach one another. The binding-site cartoon explains spatial cooperation, not an actual <enzyme> or a measured <protein structure>. No actual coordinates, physical scale or exact <protein folding> predictor are implied.}

<Protein structure> is a moving arrangement, not a permanently rigid outline. <Substrate> binding can change an <enzyme>'s shape through <induced fit>, and a functional <protein> can include flexible regions. Sequence, surroundings and interacting <molecules> must therefore be considered together. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/][Alberts et al.: binding, conformational changes and protein function]{ref}.

= Polypeptide
{parent=Protein}

= Polypeptide chain
{synonym}

A <polypeptide> is a chain of <amino acid residues> linked by <peptide bonds>. The term describes chain chemistry; calling a chain a <polypeptide> alone does not establish that it is folded or functional. The <gene expression> example N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C contains two <amino acid residues> and one <peptide bond>; the twelve-residue example under <protein> contains eleven <peptide bonds>. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/AminoAcid/A1416.html][IUPAC-IUB: amino-acid symbols and representation of residues in chains]{ref}.

= Amino acid residue
{parent=Polypeptide}

An <amino acid residue> is the portion of an <amino acid> incorporated into a <polypeptide chain>. For example, the A in the illustrative <protein primary structure> is an <alanine> residue, rather than a free <alanine> <molecule> floating beside the chain. Counting <amino acid residues> counts chain positions; repeated letters are separate positions. See \a[https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/AminoAcid/A1416.html][IUPAC-IUB, section 3AA-16: symbols for amino-acid residues]{ref}.

= Peptide bond
{parent=Polypeptide}
{wiki}
{title2=$-\mathrm{C}(=\mathrm{O})-\mathrm{NH}-$}

A <peptide bond> is the <covalent bond> between the carboxyl <carbon> of one <amino acid residue> and the amino <nitrogen> of the next. The displayed group shows the usual linkage, with the central carbon–nitrogen connection being the <peptide bond>. In an intact unbranched chain of $n$ <amino acid residues>, adjacent positions supply $n-1$ <peptide bonds>. Cutting one internal <peptide bond> produces two fragments; merely changing the chain's shape need not cut it. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: peptide bonds and protein structure]{ref}.

= Protein backbone
{parent=Polypeptide}

The <protein backbone> is the repeating chain of <chemical atoms> that connects successive <amino acid residues>, including their <peptide bonds>. The attached groups that distinguish <amino acids> project from this shared framework. <Protein folding> changes the framework's spatial arrangement while ordinarily preserving its sequence connectivity. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/][Cooper: polypeptide backbone and attached groups]{ref}.

= Protein structure
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

<Protein structure> describes both the chain's chemical organization and its spatial arrangement. Four levels answer different questions: <protein primary structure> gives the order; <protein secondary structure> gives recurring local patterns; <protein tertiary structure> gives one chain's overall arrangement; <protein quaternary structure> gives the assembly of multiple chains. These are levels of description, not four mandatory stages that every <protein> must pass through. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins][Clark, Douglas and Choi: four levels of protein structure]{ref}.

= Protein primary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

The <protein primary structure> is the ordered <amino acid residue> sequence, conventionally read from the <N-terminus> to the <C-terminus>. Thus N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C and N–<alanine>–<methionine>–C have the same composition but different <protein primary structures>. This is why a sequence preserves more information than an <amino acid> composition <histogram>. Folding an intact chain changes its shape without changing this order.

= Protein secondary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

<Protein secondary structure> consists of recurring local arrangements of the <protein backbone>, notably the <alpha helix> and <beta sheet>. <Backbone hydrogen bonds> help stabilize these patterns. A <beta sheet> can bring together stretches far apart in the <protein primary structure>; local refers to the pattern of backbone geometry, not necessarily neighboring sequence positions. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/][Alberts et al.: alpha helices, beta sheets and backbone interactions]{ref}.

= Alpha helix
{parent=Protein secondary structure}
{wiki}
{title2=$\alpha$}

An <alpha helix> is a coiled <protein secondary structure> with a repeated pattern of <backbone hydrogen bonds>. In the standard pattern, the backbone group of <amino acid residue> $i$ bonds to that of residue $i+4$: for example, positions 2 and 6. This is an additional spatial interaction, not a replacement <peptide bond> between those positions. Pauling, Corey and Branson proposed the helical arrangement in their 1951 paper by reasoning from the geometry of small <molecules>. See \a[https://williams.chemistry.gatech.edu/course_Information/6572/papers/pauling_1951_helix.pdf][Pauling, Corey and Branson, The Structure of Proteins: Two Hydrogen-Bonded Helical Configurations of the Polypeptide Chain, PNAS 37, 205–211 (1951)]{ref}.

= Beta sheet
{parent=Protein secondary structure}
{wiki}
{title2=$\beta$}

A <beta sheet> consists of extended stretches of <protein backbone> lying alongside one another, joined by <backbone hydrogen bonds>. The stretches can belong to different portions of the same <polypeptide chain> or to different chains.

= Backbone hydrogen bond
{parent=Protein secondary structure}

A <backbone hydrogen bond> is a <hydrogen bond> involving groups of the <protein backbone>, typically a backbone <oxygen> <chemical atom> and a <hydrogen> <chemical atom> already joined by a <covalent bond> to backbone <nitrogen>. It can connect positions that are not adjacent along the <polypeptide chain>. The <peptide bonds> still determine which <amino acid residues> are consecutive. See the original structural argument cited under <alpha helix>.

= Protein tertiary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

The <protein tertiary structure> is the overall spatial arrangement of one <polypeptide chain>. It includes how its <protein secondary structures> and intervening regions fit together. For example, the schematic under <protein> places positions 4 and 9 near each other despite their separation in <protein primary structure>. Such cooperation can help construct an <active site>; sequence distance and spatial separation answer different questions. <Hydrophobicity>, <backbone hydrogen bonds> and other interactions help stabilize folds, and some <proteins> additionally contain <protein disulfide bonds>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/][Cooper: tertiary structure and interactions between chain regions]{ref}.

= Protein disulfide bond
{parent=Protein tertiary structure}
{title2=$\mathrm{S-S}$}

A <protein disulfide bond> is a <disulfide bond> linking two <sulfur> <chemical atoms> carried by suitable <amino acid residues>. It can connect distant portions of a <polypeptide chain> or different chains. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/][Cooper: sulfur-containing residues and disulfide crosslinks]{ref}. The four internal <protein disulfide bonds> of <ribonuclease A> are central to the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>; breaking these crosslinks need not break the <peptide bonds> along its <protein backbone>.

= Protein quaternary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

The <protein quaternary structure> is the arrangement of multiple <polypeptide chains> in a functional assembly. A single-chain <protein> has <protein tertiary structure> without requiring <protein quaternary structure>. For an exact bookkeeping example, two intact model chains of twelve <amino acid residues> each contain $2\times12=24$ residues and $2\times11=22$ <peptide bonds>. Assembly alone adds no <peptide bond> joining their ends. Stable functional assembly should also be distinguished from <protein aggregation>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/][Alberts et al.: proteins as assemblies of polypeptide subunits]{ref}.

= Protein folding
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

<Protein folding> is the process by which a <polypeptide chain> acquires an organized spatial arrangement. The interactions of its <amino acid residues> with one another and the surroundings favor some arrangements over others. <Protein folding> can bring chemically useful groups together at an <active site> without changing their order along the <protein backbone>. Its importance is practical: knowing a sequence alone does not tell us whether a sample has the structure needed for function. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/][Cooper: acquiring functional protein structure]{ref}.

The <ribonuclease A refolding experiment> showed that an isolated <protein> could recover its native properties without a pre-existing folded template under suitable laboratory conditions. Anfinsen interpreted this through a thermodynamic hypothesis: the native arrangement minimizes the whole system's <Gibbs free energy> in the appropriate environment. His Nobel lecture was delivered on 11 December 1972 and published in Science in 1973. The specified environment is part of the claim, not a disposable qualification. See \a[https://web.iitd.ac.in/~amittal/1973_Anfinsen_Science.pdf][Anfinsen, Principles that Govern the Folding of Protein Chains, Science 181, 223–230 (1973): Nobel lecture, especially p. 223]{ref}.

This does not imply that every <protein> rapidly folds unaided. <Molecular chaperones> can assist <protein folding> and reduce <protein aggregation>; required partners, surroundings and time still matter. Goloubinoff and colleagues' 1989 reconstitution experiments demonstrated assistance from two <molecular chaperones> and <ATP> in recovering an active <enzyme> from unfolded chains. This is experimental evidence that assistance can matter even when the chains already have their specified sequences. See \a[https://www.nature.com/articles/342884a0][Goloubinoff, Christeller, Gatenby and Lorimer, 1989: chaperone-assisted reconstitution of an active enzyme]{ref}.

= Chaperone
{disambiguate=protein}
{parent=Protein folding}
{wiki}

= Molecular chaperone
{synonym}

A <molecular chaperone> is a <protein> that assists other <proteins> in reaching or maintaining useful arrangements, for example by limiting unwanted association during <protein folding>. Assistance does not rewrite the client's <protein primary structure>. In a crowded <cytoplasm>, reducing <protein aggregation> can make successful <protein folding> more reliable. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/][Cooper: molecular chaperones and folding assistance]{ref}.

= Denaturation
{disambiguate=biochemistry}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

= Protein denaturation
{synonym}

<Protein denaturation> is the loss of a <protein>'s native spatial organization, often with loss of biological function. Changes in <temperature>, <pH> or surroundings can disrupt <protein secondary structure>, <protein tertiary structure> or <protein quaternary structure>. <Protein denaturation> ordinarily preserves the <peptide bonds> and <protein primary structure>; it is different from cutting the chain. Recovery by <protein folding> is possible in some cases, but removing the denaturing conditions does not guarantee that every <protein> recovers. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins][Clark, Douglas and Choi: denaturation, environmental conditions and loss of function]{ref}.

\Image[protein-folding-and-denaturation.png]
{title=<Protein folding>, <protein denaturation>, chain cleavage and <protein aggregation> are distinct events}
{description=Original toy schematic. Folded and unfolded drawings both preserve all twelve <amino acid residues> in the same order and all eleven <peptide bonds>. Cleavage removes one internal <peptide bond>, leaving two fragments with ten remaining <peptide bonds> in total. The separate <protein aggregation> drawing contains two associated chains with eleven <peptide bonds> each. Refolding is a qualified possibility, not a universal outcome. The drawings are not actual <protein> coordinates.}

In the diagram, unfolding preserves all eleven <peptide bonds>. Cleaving the connection between positions 6 and 7 leaves two six-residue fragments: each contains five <peptide bonds>, so there are $5+5=10$ in total. Simply cooling or otherwise restoring suitable conditions cannot recover the original chain by <protein folding> alone; the missing <covalent bond> would have to be remade. Conversely, <protein aggregation> can impede recovery without breaking any <peptide bond>. These distinctions explain why loss of function does not by itself reveal whether a <protein> unfolded, associated with other chains, or suffered chemical damage.

= Protein aggregation
{parent=Denaturation (biochemistry)}
{wiki}

<Protein aggregation> is the association of <proteins> into clusters, often involving exposed regions of partly folded or unfolded <polypeptide chains>. It can prevent recovery of normal function while leaving the <protein primary structures> intact. It is distinct from both unfolding one chain and the functional assembly described by <protein quaternary structure>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/][Cooper: partly folded chains, aggregation and chaperone protection]{ref}.

= Ribonuclease A
{parent=Protein}

<Ribonuclease A> is an <enzyme> that catalyzes cleavage of <RNA>. The bovine pancreatic <ribonuclease A> studied by Anfinsen and colleagues has 124 <amino acid residues> and four internal <protein disulfide bonds>. It supplied a particularly informative test of the relationship between <protein primary structure>, <protein folding> and biological function: recovery could be measured through restored <enzyme> activity. See the primary sources under <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>.

= Ribonuclease A refolding experiment
{parent=Ribonuclease A}

Anfinsen, Haber, Sela and White's 1961 experiments began by treating bovine pancreatic <ribonuclease A> with <mercaptoethanol> in $8\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <urea>. <Urea> promotes unfolding; <mercaptoethanol> breaks <protein disulfide bonds>. The <peptide bonds> along the chain remain intact. After separating the chain from those reagents, the authors allowed its reactive <sulfur> groups to form <protein disulfide bonds> in the presence of <molecular oxygen>, at <pH> $8.2$ and <temperature> $23$–$24\,{}^{\circ}\mathrm C$. They examined <protein> <concentrations> of $0.1$ and $2.0\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}$. Measurements showed recovery of native properties under these conditions; the isolated major component had normal activity toward both <RNA> and a synthetic <substrate>. See \a[https://williams.chemistry.gatech.edu/course_Information/6572_fall_2021/papers/ANFINSEN_1961.pdf][Anfinsen, Haber, Sela and White, The Kinetics of Formation of Native Ribonuclease during Oxidation of the Reduced Polypeptide Chain, PNAS 47, 1309–1314 (1961), pp. 1309–1311]{ref}.

The observation supports the sufficiency of that intact sequence to recover native <protein structure> in the tested surroundings without a folded template. It does not establish that every <protein> refolds, that the original living <biological cell> needed no assistance, or that activity always returns immediately. <Protein folding>, forming <protein disulfide bonds> and recovering an <active site> are related processes rather than interchangeable measurements.

Anfinsen's lecture also discusses forming <protein disulfide bonds> while the chain remains in concentrated <urea>. This gave mostly inactive material; after removing <urea>, a small amount of <mercaptoethanol> permitted rearrangement toward the native form. As an exact combinatorial check, eight labeled reactive <sulfur> groups have $7\times5\times3\times1=105$ ways to be paired into four <protein disulfide bonds>: choose the partner of one group, then repeat with the unpaired groups. Only one pairing is the native one. Equal counting does not mean that all pairings are equally likely experimentally, nor that actual <protein folding> searches them uniformly. See \a[https://web.iitd.ac.in/~amittal/1973_Anfinsen_Science.pdf][Anfinsen's Nobel lecture, 1973 publication: scrambled ribonuclease and the 105 pairings, p. 223]{ref}.

= Lysozyme
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

<Lysozyme> is a <protein> <enzyme> that attacks the <peptidoglycan> of bacterial <cell walls>. Its <active site> is a cleft in the folded <protein structure>, where a suitable <substrate> can interact with positioned chemical groups. This illustrates why possessing the right <amino acid residues> is insufficient if their spatial arrangement is lost. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/][Alberts et al.: lysozyme as a worked example of protein binding and enzyme function]{ref}.

A concrete structural example is Cheetham, Artymiuk and Phillips's 1992 study of hen egg-white <lysozyme> bound to a short <carbohydrate> that inhibits its action. The bound <carbohydrate> occupies part of the <active site>; it is not a reacting <substrate> in this complex. The study describes specific interactions and changes of shape accompanying binding. Thus a real binding site has chemical interactions and flexibility as well as a geometric outline. See \a[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1569548/][Cheetham, Artymiuk and Phillips, 1992: structure of a lysozyme complex with bound inhibitor]{ref} and \a[https://www.rcsb.org/structure/1HEW][RCSB PDB entry 1HEW: the authors' structure and bound three-unit carbohydrate]{ref}. The diagrams above illustrate the idea; their twelve-residue strings are not <lysozyme> sequences or coordinates.

= N-terminus
{c}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

The N-terminus is the amino end of a <protein> or <amino acid> chain. Sequence notation lists residues from the <N-terminus> toward the <C-terminus>, the same direction in which <biological translation> builds a chain. The two-residue example under <gene expression> places <methionine> at the N-terminal end before any subsequent processing. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins][OpenStax Biology 2e, 3.4: amino acids and polypeptide direction]{ref}

= C-terminus
{c}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

The C-terminus is the carboxyl end of a <protein> or <amino acid> chain. During <biological translation>, each new residue is added at this end. The example under <gene expression> gives N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C, placing <alanine> at the <C-terminus>; its <stop codon> supplies no further residue. \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins][OpenStax Biology 2e, 3.4: polypeptide termini]{ref}

= Actin
{parent=Protein}

<Actin> is a <protein> whose monomers assemble into filaments. An idealized actin filament has a bending modulus and persistence length; inferring its effective elastic modulus from those measurements additionally requires a cross-sectional model.

= Receptor
{disambiguate=biochemistry}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

= Receptor
{synonym}

= Ionotropic receptor
{parent=Receptor (biochemistry)}

An <ionotropic receptor> is a <receptor> whose <neurotransmitter> binding directly controls an associated <ion channel>. At a <chemical synapse>, this couples chemical recognition to <synaptic conductance> without an intervening intracellular signaling cascade. See the receptor comparison under <chemical synapse>.

= Metabotropic receptor
{parent=Receptor (biochemistry)}
{wiki}

A <metabotropic receptor> acts through intracellular <signal transduction> instead of directly opening its own <ion channel>. At a <chemical synapse>, it can alter postsynaptic <ion channels> indirectly and can have effects beyond <membrane potential>. See the receptor comparison under <chemical synapse>.

= Morphogen
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <morphogen> is a developmental signal whose spatial concentration influences different <biological cell> fates or behaviors. Transport, production and degradation can establish a concentration gradient; a <morphogen reaction-diffusion equation> is one model of that gradient, not the signal itself.

= Twin
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Twins are two offspring from the same gestation. <Monozygotic twins> arise from one fertilized egg, while <dizygotic twins> arise from two. The distinction supplies the biological premise for <twin studies>.

= Biosignature
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <biosignature> is observable chemical, morphological or other evidence attributable to biological activity after assessing plausible abiotic explanations. It can concern present or past life on Earth or elsewhere. An <exoplanet biosignature> is the remote planetary specialization, requiring atmospheric and environmental context.

= Mortality
{parent=Biology}

Mortality describes deaths in a defined population during a specified observation window. A cumulative death <probability> is distinct from a death rate per person-time. Comparing treatments requires the same endpoint definition, time origin and ascertainment in both arms.

= Neuroscience
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

The study of nervous systems, from the properties of individual <neurons> to circuits, development, behavior and information processing.

= Primary visual cortex
{parent=Neuroscience}
{title2=V1}

An early cortical stage of visual processing containing neurons selective for such properties as position and orientation. Its organization combines developmental constraints and activity-dependent mechanisms.

= Correlation-based development of visual cortical maps
{parent=Primary visual cortex}

Consider afferent activities $z_i$, cortical activity $y_a$ and a constrained <Hebbian learning> rule $\dot w_{ai}=\epsilon\langle y_az_i\rangle-\Lambda_a$. If $y_a=\sum_{b,j}K_{ab}w_{bj}z_j$, then $\langle y_az_i\rangle=\sum_{b,j}K_{ab}w_{bj}C_{ji}$, where $C$ is the input correlation matrix. Thus input correlations and lateral interactions select growing weight modes, while <synaptic normalization> and bounds stop unlimited growth. The model tests how organization can emerge without assigning every final cortical feature in advance.

= Complex cell
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

A <complex cell> typically responds selectively to an oriented edge or bar over a finite spatial region with less dependence on spatial phase and contrast polarity than a <simple cell>. It need not have the simple cell's separated linear ON and OFF subregions. Phase tolerance is local, not invariance to arbitrary displacement outside its <receptive field>.

= Quadrature energy model of a complex cell
{parent=Complex cell}
{title2=$E=(k_e*I)^2+(k_o*I)^2$}

Two oriented linear filters in quadrature have responses $A\cos\varphi$ and $A\sin\varphi$ to a suitably matched narrowband grating. Their squared sum is $A^2$, independent of phase $\varphi$, while orientation and spatial-frequency selectivity remain. A nonlinear output function or normalization can transform this energy into firing rate. Ideal quadrature gives an exact phase identity for the matched stimulus; finite approximate filter pairs need not have perfect invariance for arbitrary images.

= Pooling model of a complex cell
{parent=Complex cell}

Rectify outputs of <simple cells> with similar orientations and varying phases or positions, then sum or pool them. Opposite-polarity subunits allow response to both light and dark features. Pooling gives approximate phase and position tolerance over the subunit coverage; its extent and contrast dependence depend on the pooling rule.

= Ocular dominance column
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

A cortical region whose visual responses preferentially reflect input from one eye. Competitive activity-dependent models can explain segregation of correlated inputs, while anatomical constraints and species differences also matter.

= Activity-dependent ocular dominance segregation
{parent=Ocular dominance column}

Two eye-specific afferent populations compete under <Hebbian learning> and <synaptic normalization>. In a simplified symmetric two-eye model, the eye-difference mode $d_a=w_{aL}-w_{aR}$ evolves with a linear operator containing $K_{ab}(C_{\rm same}-C_{\rm cross})_{ab}$. Dominant spatial modes can produce alternating eye preference, while saturation sets finite contrast. The interaction width, arbor constraints and correlation structure influence the column scale. This is a mechanistic hypothesis whose predictions can be tested, not a unique inference from the existence of columns.

= Orientation selectivity
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

Dependence of a visual neuron's response on stimulus orientation. Elongated ON/OFF receptive-field organization can make aligned bars or edges produce stronger drive than orthogonal ones.

= Orientation-map self-organization
{parent=Orientation selectivity}

Correlation-based learning among spatially arranged ON and OFF inputs, with <synaptic competition> and lateral interactions, can select elongated <simple cell> receptive fields. Neighbor interactions coordinate preferred orientations across cortex, while competition allows different orientations to persist. Selectivity within one cell and a spatial map across many cells are separate predictions, and neither follows from unbounded <Hebbian learning> alone.

= Simple cell
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

A visual cortical neuron with spatially distinct ON and OFF subregions, commonly elongated along a preferred orientation. A signed oriented filter followed by a nonlinearity gives a useful simplified model of its response to bars or gratings.

= Neuron
{parent=Neuroscience}
{wiki}

= Nerve cell
{synonym}

A <neuron> is a specialized <biological cell> that receives, integrates and transmits signals. Its <membrane potential> is the <electric potential difference> between its interior and exterior. Inputs can produce graded changes in <membrane potential>; many <neurons> also generate <action potentials> that carry signals along an <axon> to <synapses>. Some <neurons> communicate without generating <action potentials>.

A common structural plan has branching <dendrites> that receive inputs, a <soma> (cell body) containing the <cell nucleus>, and an <axon> leading to output sites. The <cell membrane> surrounds all these parts. In many <neurons>, <action potentials> begin near the <soma> in the <axon initial segment>. This is a useful plan rather than a universal shape or an absolute rule about where inputs arrive. See \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch1.S1.html][Gerstner and colleagues: elements of neuronal systems]{ref} and \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/][Alberts and colleagues: electrical properties of membranes]{ref}.

A worked integration example uses the <leaky integrate-and-fire model>: two simultaneous inward <electric currents> of $100\,\mathrm{pA}$ give a total $200\,\mathrm{pA}$. With the illustrative parameters below, either input alone leaves the <membrane potential> below the firing threshold, whereas both sustained together produce <action potential> events. Inputs separated in time need not have the same effect, because earlier changes in <membrane potential> decay. Real <dendrites> add spatial filtering and potentially nonlinear responses, so this simple sum is a model assumption.

\Image[neuron-signal-path.png]
{title=From neuronal inputs to a chemical synapse: an original mechanism schematic}
{description=The upper panel identifies <dendrites>, the <soma>, the <cell nucleus>, the <axon initial segment> and the <axon>. The lower panel enlarges a <chemical synapse>: an arriving <action potential> opens <voltage-gated ion channels> for <calcium>, promoting release of <neurotransmitter> from <synaptic vesicles>; a <receptor> changes the receiving cell's <electric current>. This is an illustrative structure and mechanism, not a photograph or a drawing to scale.}

The combination of local integration and transmission makes <neurons> useful building blocks for <neural coding>: activity can depend on both the current input and recent inputs. Changes at <synapses> through <synaptic plasticity> can alter that input-output relationship.

= Soma
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Neuron}
{wiki}

= Soma
{synonym}

= Cell body
{synonym}

= Neural adaptation
{parent=Neuron}
{wiki}

Changes in responsiveness during sustained or repeated input. Adaptation makes activity depend on recent stimulation and can invalidate a model whose rate depends only on the present stimulus.

= Synapse
{parent=Neuron}
{wiki}

A <synapse> is a signaling junction between a <neuron> and another <biological cell>. The sending side is presynaptic and the receiving side postsynaptic. At a <chemical synapse>, released <neurotransmitter> acts on a <receptor> in the receiving <cell membrane>. At an <electrical synapse>, <gap junctions> provide a direct path for <electric current> between cells. See \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch1.S1.html][Gerstner and colleagues: chemical and electrical synapses]{ref}.

In a common sequence at a <chemical synapse>, an arriving <action potential> opens <voltage-gated ion channels> selective for <calcium>. <Calcium> entry triggers fusion of <synaptic vesicles> with the presynaptic <cell membrane>, releasing <neurotransmitter> into the <synaptic cleft>. Binding to a <receptor> changes postsynaptic <electrical resistance and conductance>, directly through an <ion channel> or through an intracellular signaling pathway. The resulting change in <membrane potential> is a <synaptic potential>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/][Alberts and colleagues: chemical synaptic transmission]{ref}.

A useful conductance approximation writes the inward-positive synaptic <electric current> as
$$
I_{\mathrm{syn}}=g_{\mathrm{syn}}(t)(E_{\mathrm{syn}}-V),
$$
where $g_{\mathrm{syn}}\geq0$ describes the activated <electrical resistance and conductance>, $V$ is the postsynaptic <membrane potential>, and $E_{\mathrm{syn}}$ is the <reversal potential>, the voltage at which this current vanishes. Opening these channels tends to pull $V$ toward $E_{\mathrm{syn}}$; it does not prescribe a current of fixed sign independently of $V$.

For a worked example, let $V=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $g_{\mathrm{syn}}=1\,\mathrm{nS}$. A <reversal potential> of $0\,\mathrm{mV}$ gives $I_{\mathrm{syn}}=65\,\mathrm{pA}$, an inward <electric current> that raises <membrane potential>. A <reversal potential> of $-75\,\mathrm{mV}$ instead gives $-10\,\mathrm{pA}$, which lowers <membrane potential>. These are illustrative calculations, not measurements. A <reversal potential> of $-60\,\mathrm{mV}$ can raise $V$ from $-65\,\mathrm{mV}$ yet oppose reaching a threshold of $-50\,\mathrm{mV}$: a depolarizing input need not be excitatory.

An excitatory <synapse> favors a subsequent <action potential>, whereas an inhibitory <synapse> opposes it. The effect depends on the <receptor>, the permeating <ions>, their gradients and the <reversal potential>; a <neurotransmitter> does not have one fixed excitatory or inhibitory sign in every context. Even with $E_{\mathrm{syn}}=V$, an added conductance can reduce responses to other inputs by lowering effective <electrical resistance>, an effect called <shunting inhibition>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11117/][Purves and colleagues: excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials]{ref}. This dependence matters when translating a biological <synapse> into a signed weight or an imposed <electric current> in <computational neuroscience>.

= Synaptic plasticity
{parent=Synapse}
{wiki}

Changes in synaptic efficacy over time. Activity-dependent plasticity can support learning and development, but also requires constraints to prevent unbounded growth or competition in simplified models.

= Synaptic competition
{parent=Synaptic plasticity}

Constraints or interactions make the strengthening of some inputs limit others. In developmental models, competition together with input correlations can segregate ON and OFF subregions or inputs from the two eyes; correlations alone do not specify the full biological developmental mechanism.

= Synaptic normalization
{parent=Synaptic competition}

A learning rule can constrain the sum or norm of incoming synaptic weights to prevent unbounded <Hebbian learning>. For $\sum_iw_{ai}=S$, subtracting the mean unconstrained update from each update preserves the sum. Bounds and renormalization may also be needed to keep excitatory weights nonnegative. Normalization of learning weights is distinct from divisive gain control in instantaneous neural responses.

= Neurotransmitter
{parent=Synapse}
{wiki}

A chemical signal released by a neuron that changes a target cell's activity through its receptors. Its effect depends on the receptor and ionic mechanism rather than being intrinsically excitatory or inhibitory in every context.

= Chemical synapse
{parent=Synapse}
{wiki}

A <chemical synapse> transmits a signal by releasing <neurotransmitter> from a presynaptic <neuron> to act on postsynaptic <receptors> across a <synaptic cleft>. The two <cell membranes> remain separate. A typical fast transmission sequence is:

* An arriving <action potential> produces presynaptic <depolarization>, opening <voltage-gated ion channels> selective for <calcium>.
* <Calcium> <ions> enter down their <electrochemical gradient>. The local rise in <calcium> <concentration> activates release machinery, allowing already prepared <synaptic vesicles> to fuse with the presynaptic <cell membrane> by <exocytosis>.
* Released <neurotransmitter> crosses the <synaptic cleft> by <diffusion> and binds postsynaptic <receptors>.
* <Ionotropic receptors> directly gate an <ion channel>; <metabotropic receptors> activate an intracellular signaling pathway that can alter <ion channels> indirectly. Changed <synaptic conductance> changes <electric current> and usually produces a <synaptic potential>.

See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11009/][Purves and colleagues, Neuroscience: chemical synapses]{ref} and \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch3.S1.html][Gerstner and colleagues, Neuronal Dynamics: receptor mechanisms]{ref}. The fast pathway uses <synaptic vesicles> prepared near release sites; it does not require a distant <synaptic vesicle> to travel across the terminal after every <action potential>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK27911/][Basic Neurochemistry: calcium entry and vesicles at release sites]{ref}. Some <chemical synapses> release <neurotransmitter> in response to graded presynaptic <membrane potential> changes rather than <action potentials>.

\Image[chemical-synapse-transmission.png]
{title=From an <action potential> to <neurotransmitter> release and postsynaptic <electric current>}
{description=Original schematic of a typical fast <chemical synapse>, not to scale. <Calcium> entry occurs on the presynaptic side; <neurotransmitter> crosses the <synaptic cleft>. The postsynaptic <ionotropic receptor> gates its associated <ion channel>. The inset contrasts direct <ionotropic receptor> gating with indirect <metabotropic receptor> signaling. Arrows indicate mechanisms, not measured timing or amounts; <neurotransmitter clearance> and <synaptic vesicle> recycling are distinct processes.}

<Neurotransmitter clearance> limits how long released <neurotransmitter> remains available. Depending on the <neurotransmitter> and location, <diffusion> away, uptake through <membrane transport proteins> into the sending or neighboring <biological cells>, and breakdown by <enzymes> contribute. <Receptor> binding and unbinding also affect response duration. Recovering <synaptic vesicle> membrane is a separate recycling task; uptake of released <neurotransmitter> is not the return of an intact <synaptic vesicle>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26813/][Alberts and colleagues: local signaling and transmitter removal]{ref}.

This electrical-to-chemical-to-electrical sequence offers several places to adjust a signal: <synaptic release probability>, <receptor> availability and <neurotransmitter clearance> can change its strength or duration. It helps explain why <synaptic plasticity> can modify communication between <neurons>, and why an arriving <action potential> need not cause a postsynaptic <action potential>.

Otto Loewi's 1936 Nobel lecture describes his 1921 experiment: fluid from a frog heart whose nerves had been stimulated produced the corresponding response when transferred to another heart. The transferable substance established chemical mediation in that preparation. This was evidence for released chemical signaling, not yet an identification of <synaptic vesicle> fusion and not a claim that every <synapse> is chemical. See \a[https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1936/loewi/lecture/][Loewi, The Chemical Transmission of Nerve Action, Nobel lecture, 12 December 1936]{ref}.

= Neurotransmitter clearance
{parent=Chemical synapse}

<Neurotransmitter clearance> removes released <neurotransmitter> from the region where it can activate <receptors>, through <diffusion>, cellular uptake or <enzyme>-mediated breakdown. Its routes and rates help delimit a <chemical synapse>'s response in space and time; see the mechanisms and source under <chemical synapse>.

= Synaptic cleft
{parent=Chemical synapse}

The synaptic cleft is the extracellular gap between the sending and receiving <cell membranes> of a <chemical synapse>. Released <neurotransmitter> crosses this gap to reach a <receptor>. \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/][Alberts and colleagues, Molecular Biology of the Cell: chemical synaptic transmission]{ref}

= Synaptic vesicle
{parent=Chemical synapse}
{wiki}

A <synaptic vesicle> is a small membrane-bounded compartment in a presynaptic terminal that stores <neurotransmitter> for release. Fusion with the <cell membrane> exposes its contents to the <synaptic cleft>; the packet supplied by one <synaptic vesicle> is a quantum of release. <Quantal neurotransmitter release> refers to these discrete packets, rather than the release of one <molecule> at a time. The postsynaptic response to a packet can vary with its contents, <receptors> and recording conditions. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10866/][Purves and colleagues: vesicles as the source of transmitter quanta]{ref}.

The useful cycle is filling, preparation for fusion, <calcium>-triggered <exocytosis>, and recovery of membrane and <proteins> through <endocytosis> for reuse. Different recovery routes occur; recycling need not preserve a particular <synaptic vesicle> as an identifiable container throughout. Limited availability of prepared <synaptic vesicles> can reduce release during repeated stimulation, while changes in <synaptic release probability> can also change successive responses. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK27911/][Basic Neurochemistry: secretion machinery and vesicle reuse]{ref} and \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch3.S1.html][Gerstner and colleagues: short-term synaptic plasticity]{ref}.

For an illustrative calculation, assume four release sites, each with one prepared <synaptic vesicle>, each releasing with <synaptic release probability> $p=0.25$ during one presynaptic <action potential>. Assume <independent> <Bernoulli trials> and at most one release per site. The released packet count $K$ has a <binomial distribution>:
$$
K\sim\operatorname{Bin}(4,0.25),\qquad \mathbb E[K]=4(0.25)=1,\qquad \operatorname{Var}(K)=4(0.25)(0.75)=0.75.
$$
The <probability> of no release is $\mathbb P(K=0)=0.75^4=0.31640625$; at least one release has <probability> $0.68359375$. An <expected value> of one packet does not mean that every <action potential> releases one packet. If each packet contributes a stipulated $0.5\,\mathrm{nS}$ conductance increment and increments add, the <expected value> of that increment is $0.5\,\mathrm{nS}$, with zero increment on about $31.6\%$ of trials. These assumptions are a teaching model: shared <calcium> fluctuations, unequal sites, depletion and nonlinear <receptor> responses can invalidate them.

= Quantal neurotransmitter release
{parent=Synaptic vesicle}
{wiki}

<Quantal neurotransmitter release> is release of <neurotransmitter> in discrete packets associated with individual <synaptic vesicles>. Packet count and postsynaptic packet response are different quantities; see the <binomial distribution> example under <synaptic vesicle>.

= Synaptic release probability
{parent=Quantal neurotransmitter release}
{title2=$p$}

<Synaptic release probability> is the <probability> of release at a specified site during a specified opportunity, such as one presynaptic <action potential>. It depends on the site's state and conditions; it is not the <probability> that the receiving <neuron> fires. In the example under <synaptic vesicle>, $p$ refers to one available site, whereas $1-(1-p)^4$ refers to any of four <independent> sites releasing.

= Shunting inhibition
{parent=Chemical synapse}
{wiki}

<Shunting inhibition> is suppression of another input's effect through increased <synaptic conductance>: opening additional <ion channels> lowers effective <electrical resistance>, reducing the voltage response to a given added <electric current>. When the added <reversal potential> equals the resting <membrane potential>, there can be no voltage change at rest and still be inhibition of a simultaneous input. See \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch1.S4.html][Gerstner and colleagues: shunting inhibition and reversal potential]{ref}.

Use the passive <synaptic potential> model below with leak $g_L=10\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_L=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$ and <capacitance> $C=200\,\mathrm{pF}$. A sustained inward test <electric current> of $I_{\mathrm{ext}}=100\,\mathrm{pA}$ alone gives a steady voltage increase of $I_{\mathrm{ext}}/g_L=10\,\mathrm{mV}$, so $V_\infty=-55\,\mathrm{mV}$. Effective <electrical resistance> is $100\,\mathrm{M\Omega}$ and the <time constant> is $20\,\mathrm{ms}$.

Now hold a shunting conductance $g_i=20\,\mathrm{nS}$ open with $E_i=E_L=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$. With no test <electric current>, rest stays at $-65\,\mathrm{mV}$. With the same $100\,\mathrm{pA}$ test <electric current>, total conductance becomes $30\,\mathrm{nS}$, so the steady voltage increase is only $100/30\simeq3.333\,\mathrm{mV}$: $V_\infty\simeq-61.667\,\mathrm{mV}$. Effective <electrical resistance> is $33.333\,\mathrm{M\Omega}$ and the <time constant> is $200/30\simeq6.667\,\mathrm{ms}$. A faster response can therefore be smaller. At the original rest the shunting <electric current> is zero; at $V=-55\,\mathrm{mV}$ it is $20(-65+55)=-200\,\mathrm{pA}$, opposing the voltage excursion.

The factor of three reduction applies to this constant-<electric current>, constant-conductance example. A finite pulse, conductance-driven input or spatially extended <dendrite> need not have the same reduction. The interesting feature is conditional action: the shunt can leave a quiet <neuron>'s voltage unchanged yet reshape its response when another input arrives. A model that represents all inhibition as a fixed outward <electric current> misses this behavior.

= Electrical synapse
{parent=Synapse}
{wiki}

An <electrical synapse> passes ionic <electric current> directly between <neurons> through <gap junctions>, whose paired channels connect the two <cytoplasms>. It does not require <synaptic vesicle> release and postsynaptic <neurotransmitter> binding. Transmission across the junction is very fast; the receiving <cell membrane>'s <capacitance> and other conductances still determine its voltage response. Electrical coupling can coordinate the timing of activity across <neurons>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11164/][Purves and colleagues: electrical synapses and gap junctions]{ref}.

For a passive junction with constant conductance $g_j$, the inward <electric current> into cell 2 is
$$
I_{2\leftarrow1}=g_j(V_1-V_2),\qquad I_{1\leftarrow2}=-I_{2\leftarrow1},
$$
where each $V_k$ is the cell's <membrane potential>, measured against a common extracellular reference. Taking $g_j=2\,\mathrm{nS}$, $V_1=-55\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $V_2=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$ gives $20\,\mathrm{pA}$ into cell 2 and the equal opposite <electric current> into cell 1. Exchanging the voltages reverses the <electric current>. Both <depolarization> and <hyperpolarization> can therefore be transmitted; an <electrical synapse> is not inherently an excitatory connection.

Many <electrical synapses> conduct in both directions, but this does not guarantee equal voltage responses: the cells can have different effective <electrical resistance> and <capacitance>. Some junctions rectify, meaning they conduct preferentially in one direction, so the constant-$g_j$ formula is insufficient. A concrete molecular example is the original study \a[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7455501/][Shui and colleagues, 2020: Molecular basis of junctional current rectification at an electrical synapse]{ref}. Bidirectionality is thus a common property with important qualifications, rather than part of the definition.

= Synaptic potential
{parent=Synapse}
{wiki}

A <synaptic potential> is a postsynaptic change in <membrane potential> caused by activity at a <synapse>. Unlike the regenerative <action potential>, a passive <synaptic potential> is graded: its size depends on input strength, the initial <membrane potential> and the receiving <cell membrane>'s properties. Local voltage changes can decay before reaching the <soma>; their timing and overlap help determine whether an <action potential> follows. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/][Alberts and colleagues: graded postsynaptic signals and integration]{ref}.

<Depolarization> means that the interior becomes less negative relative to the exterior; <hyperpolarization> means that it becomes more negative. Excitation and inhibition instead describe whether an input promotes or opposes postsynaptic <action potential> generation. An <inhibitory postsynaptic potential> can be depolarizing, and <shunting inhibition> can occur with no voltage change at rest. The <receptor>, permeating <ions>, ionic gradients and <reversal potential> matter. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11117/][Purves and colleagues: excitation, inhibition and depolarizing inhibition]{ref}.

A worked passive model treats the receiving membrane as one compartment with <capacitance> $C>0$, leak conductance $g_L>0$, and activated synaptic conductances $g_e,g_i\geq0$:
$$
C\frac{dV}{dt}=g_L(E_L-V)+g_e(E_e-V)+g_i(E_i-V)+I_{\mathrm{ext}}.
$$
All <electric currents> are inward-positive. Each term $g(E-V)$ vanishes at its <reversal potential> $E$; $\mathrm{nS}\times\mathrm{mV}=\mathrm{pA}$. With constant parameters, the <ordinary differential equation> has
$$
G=g_L+g_e+g_i,\qquad V_\infty=\frac{g_LE_L+g_eE_e+g_iE_i+I_{\mathrm{ext}}}{G},\qquad \tau=\frac{C}{G},
$$
$$
V(t)=V_\infty+(V(0)-V_\infty)e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
Here $G$ is total conductance, $1/G$ is effective <electrical resistance>, $V_\infty$ is the <steady state> and $\tau$ the <time constant>. Without imposed <electric current>, $V_\infty$ is a conductance-weighted average of the <reversal potentials>. This approximation omits spatial structure, changing ionic gradients and active <voltage-gated ion channels>; it calculates passive voltage responses, not actual <action potential> waveforms. See \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch3.S1.html][Gerstner and colleagues: conductance-based synaptic models]{ref} for the mechanism behind the terms; the inward-positive convention here reverses that source's synaptic-current sign.

Take the same illustrative leak and <capacitance> as under <shunting inhibition>, start at $V(0)=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$ and set $I_{\mathrm{ext}}=0$. Opening $g_e=2\,\mathrm{nS}$ with $E_e=0\,\mathrm{mV}$ gives $V_\infty=-650/12\simeq-54.167\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $\tau\simeq16.667\,\mathrm{ms}$. Its initial inward <electric current> is $2(0+65)=130\,\mathrm{pA}$. Adding $g_i=20\,\mathrm{nS}$ with $E_i=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$ changes $V_\infty$ to $(-650-1300)/32=-60.9375\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $\tau=6.25\,\mathrm{ms}$. This is <shunting inhibition> of the same excitatory drive.

For comparison, $g_i=20\,\mathrm{nS}$ with $E_i=-75\,\mathrm{mV}$ alone gives $V_\infty\simeq-71.667\,\mathrm{mV}$: <hyperpolarization>. With $E_i=-60\,\mathrm{mV}$ instead, it alone gives $V_\infty\simeq-61.667\,\mathrm{mV}$: <depolarization>. Yet adding this depolarizing conductance to the $g_e=2\,\mathrm{nS}$ excitatory drive lowers that drive's $V_\infty$ from $-54.167$ to $(-650-1200)/32=-57.8125\,\mathrm{mV}$. At an illustrative firing threshold $V=-50\,\mathrm{mV}$, its <electric current> is $20(-60+50)=-200\,\mathrm{pA}$, opposing threshold approach. The combined trace initially rises faster than the excitatory drive alone, before settling below it. Thus the sign of a voltage change at rest alone does not determine excitation or inhibition; a claim about firing must specify input strength, timing and the <neuron>'s state.

\Image[synaptic-conductance-and-shunting.png]
{title=<Reversal potential>, passive <synaptic potentials> and <shunting inhibition>}
{description=Computed illustrations, not recordings. Left: inward <electric current> for a $1\,\mathrm{nS}$ conductance with four <reversal potentials>. Upper right: constant-conductance steps at time zero in the one-compartment model with $C=200\,\mathrm{pF}$, $g_L=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ and $E_L=V(0)=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$. Lower right: the same $100\,\mathrm{pA}$ test <electric current> with and without $20\,\mathrm{nS}$ of added conductance at $E_i=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$. All plotted trajectories are passive; no <action potential> generation or reset is included.}

= Synaptic conductance
{parent=Synaptic potential}
{title2=$g_{\mathrm{syn}}$}

<Synaptic conductance> describes how strongly activated synaptic <ion channels> conduct <electric current> for a given driving force. In the passive approximation, $I_{\mathrm{syn}}=g_{\mathrm{syn}}(E_{\mathrm{syn}}-V)$, with inward-positive <electric current>. It is the <electrical conductance> contribution of a <synapse>; its postsynaptic voltage response also depends on the driving force, the other membrane <electrical conductances>, and the <capacitance>. The <synaptic potential> model and <shunting inhibition> example show why a nonzero <synaptic conductance> can have zero <electric current> at rest.

= Excitatory postsynaptic potential
{parent=Synaptic potential}
{wiki}

= EPSP
{c}
{synonym}

An <excitatory postsynaptic potential> is a <synaptic potential> associated with increased likelihood of a postsynaptic <action potential>. Its size and effect depend on the receiving <neuron>'s state; the definition concerns firing, not a fixed <neurotransmitter> identity. See <synaptic potential> for the mechanism and source.

= Inhibitory postsynaptic potential
{parent=Synaptic potential}
{wiki}

= IPSP
{c}
{synonym}

An <inhibitory postsynaptic potential> is a <synaptic potential> associated with decreased likelihood of a postsynaptic <action potential>. It can involve <hyperpolarization> or <depolarization>; an inhibitory conductance can even produce no measurable voltage change at rest. See <synaptic potential> and <shunting inhibition> for worked examples and sources.

= Action potential
{parent=Neuron}
{wiki}

An <action potential> is a brief regenerative excursion of <membrane potential>, produced by changing <electrical resistance and conductance> through <voltage-gated ion channels>. In a typical fast neuronal <action potential>, <sodium> entry raises <membrane potential>, activating further <sodium> channels. This feedback produces the rapid rising phase. Inactivation of <sodium> channels and increased conductance to <potassium> then lower <membrane potential> again; persisting <potassium> conductance can produce an undershoot below the resting level. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10897/][Purves and colleagues: the ionic basis of action potentials]{ref}.

For an illustrative electrical calculation, write inward <sodium> <electric current> as $I_{\mathrm{Na}}=g_{\mathrm{Na}}(E_{\mathrm{Na}}-V)$. At $V=-50\,\mathrm{mV}$, taking $E_{\mathrm{Na}}=60\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $g_{\mathrm{Na}}=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ gives $I_{\mathrm{Na}}=1.1\,\mathrm{nA}$ inward. A <potassium> current $I_{\mathrm{K}}=g_{\mathrm{K}}(E_{\mathrm{K}}-V)$ with $E_{\mathrm{K}}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $g_{\mathrm{K}}=20\,\mathrm{nS}$ gives $-0.6\,\mathrm{nA}$, outward. These illustrative values expose opposing driving forces; a waveform requires the conductances to evolve with <membrane potential> and time, as in the <Hodgkin-Huxley model>.

An <action potential> propagates along an <axon> because local <electric currents> bring neighboring regions of <cell membrane> into the regime where their <voltage-gated ion channels> regenerate the signal. It is a traveling pattern of excitation, not a packet of <sodium> <ions> traversing the entire <axon>. Regeneration allows an <action potential> to travel without the passive attenuation of a small local voltage change. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/][Alberts and colleagues: propagation of action potentials]{ref}.

The <neuronal refractory period> constrains how soon another <action potential> can follow. Under comparable conditions, successive <action potentials> have roughly stereotyped shapes, making event count and timing useful for <neural coding>; waveform details can nevertheless change with channel state and conditions. The <leaky integrate-and-fire model> abstracts each <action potential> to a threshold event and reset. Its reset jump is a mathematical rule, not the actual regenerative waveform produced by <sodium> and <potassium> currents.

= Neuronal refractory period
{parent=Action potential}

After an <action potential>, channel inactivation and recovery temporarily suppress subsequent firing. An absolute refractory interval forbids another spike; a relative interval requires stronger excitation. A memoryless Poisson spike model omits this dependence on recent spikes.

= Dendrite
{parent=Neuron}
{wiki}

A neuronal process that receives and integrates many inputs. Its geometry and membrane properties can make integration spatially and temporally selective.

= Axon
{parent=Neuron}
{wiki}

A neuronal process that carries signals toward output sites, often by propagation of <action potentials> to <synapses>.

= Axon initial segment
{parent=Axon}

The axon initial segment is the specialized proximal region of an <axon> near the <soma>. In many <neurons>, its <voltage-gated ion channels> make it the site where an <action potential> begins. \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch1.S1.html][Gerstner and colleagues, Neuronal Dynamics: axon initial segment]{ref}

= Computational neuroscience
{parent=Neuroscience}
{wiki}

Mathematical and computational models connect neural mechanisms, measured responses and proposed information-processing functions. Their predictions require comparison with experiments; matching observations does not uniquely identify the biological mechanism.

= Dynamic clamp
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{wiki}

A <dynamic clamp> measures a real neuron's voltage, updates a conductance model in real time and injects the resulting current, for example $I=g(t)(E-V)$. It implements a virtual conductance without holding the voltage fixed. It can add artificial synapses, couple real neurons into a hybrid network or test how a voltage-dependent conductance affects excitability. Sampling delay and the injection site limit fidelity to spatially distributed biological channels.

= Three-neuron ring stability
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{title2=$(1+\tau\lambda)^3=\Gamma$}

For a directed three-neuron rate ring $\tau\dot x_i=-x_i+W_{i,i-1}\tanh(\beta x_{i-1})$, the linearization has characteristic equation $(1+\tau\lambda)^3=\Gamma$, where $\Gamma=\beta^3W_{13}W_{32}W_{21}$. Positive loop gain has strict linear asymptotic stability for $\Gamma<1$. Negative loop gain has it for $-8<\Gamma<0$; at $\Gamma=-8$ a complex pair crosses the imaginary axis at frequency $\sqrt3/\tau$. The saturating cubic gives a <supercritical Hopf bifurcation> under an ordinary loop-gain variation. This directed system does not inherit the energy-descent property of a symmetric <Hopfield network>.

= Leaky integrate-and-fire model
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{title2=$C\dot V=-g_L(V-E_L)+I(t)$}

= LIF model
{c}
{synonym}

The <leaky integrate-and-fire model> combines a passive equation for <membrane potential> with a firing rule. Between events, inward-positive applied <electric current> $I(t)$ obeys
$$
C\frac{dV}{dt}=-g_L(V-E_L)+I(t),\qquad R=\frac{1}{g_L},\qquad \tau=\frac{C}{g_L}=RC,
$$
where $C>0$ is membrane <capacitance>, $g_L>0$ is the leak conductance, $R$ is the corresponding <electrical resistance>, and $E_L$ is the leak <reversal potential>. Here the leak conductance is constant. The <time constant> $\tau$ describes passive relaxation. When $V$ first reaches a threshold $V_\theta$ from below, record an <action potential> event and reset $V$ to $V_r<V_\theta$. In the version used here, hold $V=V_r$ for a fixed <neuronal refractory period> $t_{\mathrm{ref}}$, then resume integration. See \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch1.S3.html][Gerstner and colleagues: integrate-and-fire models]{ref}.

For constant $I$, with $t=0$ at the start of an integration interval and $V(0)=V_{\mathrm{initial}}$, the exact subthreshold solution is
$$
V(t)=E_L+RI+(V_{\mathrm{initial}}-E_L-RI)e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
The <equilibrium point> of this passive equation is $V_\infty=E_L+RI$. Subtracting $V_\infty$ leaves $d(V-V_\infty)/dt=-(V-V_\infty)/\tau$, explaining the <exponential function>. This solution applies only until a threshold event or a change in $I$. Starting below threshold, constant input produces repeated events if $V_\infty>V_\theta$; if $V_\infty=V_\theta$, the threshold is approached asymptotically and never reached at finite time.

For a worked example, choose
$$
E_L=V_r=V_{\mathrm{initial}}=-65\,\mathrm{mV},\quad V_\theta=-50\,\mathrm{mV},\quad \tau=20\,\mathrm{ms},\quad R=100\,\mathrm{M\Omega},\quad t_{\mathrm{ref}}=2\,\mathrm{ms}.
$$
These imply $g_L=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ and $C=200\,\mathrm{pF}$. All these parameters are illustrative model choices, not measurements from a particular <neuron>. With $I=200\,\mathrm{pA}$, <Ohm's law> gives $RI=20\,\mathrm{mV}$ and hence $V_\infty=-45\,\mathrm{mV}$. Thus
$$
V(t)=-45\,\mathrm{mV}-20\,\mathrm{mV}\,e^{-t/(20\,\mathrm{ms})}.
$$
At threshold, $e^{-t/\tau}=1/4$, so the first event occurs after $T=20\ln4\,\mathrm{ms}\approx27.7259\,\mathrm{ms}$. Each subsequent cycle includes the $2\,\mathrm{ms}$ <neuronal refractory period>, giving
$$
f=\frac{1}{T+t_{\mathrm{ref}}}\approx33.6407\,\mathrm{Hz}.
$$
This is the ideal sustained firing rate; the first-event latency is $T$, not $T+t_{\mathrm{ref}}$.

With $I=100\,\mathrm{pA}$ instead, $RI=10\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $V_\infty=-55\,\mathrm{mV}$. From the same initial state, $V(t)=-55\,\mathrm{mV}-10\,\mathrm{mV}\,e^{-t/\tau}$ stays below $-50\,\mathrm{mV}$: no events occur, however long this current is held. The critical sustained current is $(V_\theta-E_L)/R=150\,\mathrm{pA}$; equality gives asymptotic approach rather than finite-time firing.

\Image[integrate-and-fire-current-steps.png]
{title=Exact leaky integrate-and-fire responses to two current steps}
{description=Both inputs start at $20\,\mathrm{ms}$ and end at $180\,\mathrm{ms}$. A $100\,\mathrm{pA}$ step approaches $-55\,\mathrm{mV}$ without firing; a $200\,\mathrm{pA}$ step produces threshold events separated by $29.7259\,\mathrm{ms}$ after the first. Orange ticks mark events, and shaded intervals mark the $2\,\mathrm{ms}$ reset hold. The dashed $-45\,\mathrm{mV}$ level is the stronger input's passive equilibrium, which the reset trajectory never reaches. These are analytic model trajectories, not recordings or biological spike waveforms.}

The leak gives old inputs progressively less influence, while <capacitance> prevents an ordinary finite <electric current> step from changing $V$ instantly. This combination makes the <leaky integrate-and-fire model> useful for exploring input summation, event timing and network activity at low computational cost. Its fixed parameters and reset omit the evolving <voltage-gated ion channels> of the <Hodgkin-Huxley model>, spatial integration in <dendrites> and mechanisms of <neural adaptation>. Treating a <synapse> as a prescribed $I(t)$ also omits its voltage-dependent driving force unless a conductance term is added. See \a[https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch1.S4.html][Gerstner and colleagues: limitations of the leaky integrate-and-fire model]{ref} and <constant-input firing rate of a leaky integrate-and-fire neuron>.

= Constant-input firing rate of a leaky integrate-and-fire neuron
{parent=Leaky integrate-and-fire model}

For $C_m\dot V=-g_L(V-V_L)+I$, reset $V_0<V_\theta$ and no explicit refractory interval, define $I_c=g_L(V_\theta-V_L)$ and $\tau_m=C_m/g_L$. Constant current gives
$$
r(I)=\begin{cases}0,&I\leq I_c,\\\bigl[\tau_m\log((I-g_L(V_0-V_L))/(I-I_c))\bigr]^{-1},&I>I_c.\end{cases}
$$
The threshold is approached only asymptotically at $I=I_c$. At large current, $r\sim I/[C_m(V_\theta-V_0)]$. Adding an absolute <neuronal refractory period> changes the denominator to $T(I)+t_{\rm ref}$ and makes the firing rate saturate rather than grow without bound.

= Hodgkin-Huxley model
{c}
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{wiki}

A conductance-based model couples <membrane potential> to voltage-dependent channel-gating variables. It explains generation of <action potentials> through regenerative and opposing ionic currents, while its parameters and channel assumptions determine its biological domain of validity.

= Mutually excitatory saturating rate network
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{title2=$\tau\dot x=-x+g(y),\quad g(z)=ma^2z^2/(\sigma^2+a^2z^2)$}

The symmetric two-neuron rate model has nonnegative activity and saturating recurrent input. Every equilibrium has $x=y$: otherwise monotonicity of $g$ gives a contradiction. With positive parameters it has three distinct equilibria exactly when $am>2\sigma$, namely zero and $z_\pm=(m\pm\sqrt{m^2-4\sigma^2/a^2})/2$. The lower positive equilibrium is a saddle and the origin and upper equilibrium are stable nodes. The resulting two attracting activity levels model a persistent switch rather than a spiking waveform.

= Linear-nonlinear-Poisson cascade model
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{title2=$r(t)=g(b+(k*s)(t))$}
{wiki}

= LNP model
{c}
{synonym}

A stimulus passes through a linear temporal or spatial filter, then a nonnegative nonlinear rate function. A conditionally <inhomogeneous Poisson process> generates spikes at that rate. The filter and nonlinearity can be fitted from data and tested on held-out trials. Refractoriness and spike-history correlations require extensions beyond this basic cascade.

= Poisson spike-train likelihood fitting
{parent=Linear-nonlinear-Poisson cascade model}
{title2=$\ell=\sum_i\log\lambda(t_i)-\int\lambda(t)\,dt$}

For a stimulus-conditioned <inhomogeneous Poisson process>, the event log-likelihood is the displayed expression. A causal filter expansion $\lambda(t)=\exp[b+\sum_aw_a(s*b_a)(t)]$ can be fitted by <maximum likelihood estimation>, with a smoothness or size penalty and held-out validation. The integral term penalizes predicted spikes that did not occur; fitting only stimulus values at observed spikes omits this essential part.

= Spike-triggered average
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{title2=$\widehat k(u)=K^{-1}\sum_i s(t_i-u)$}
{wiki}

= STA
{c}
{synonym}

Average the stimulus history preceding each observed spike, after removing the stimulus mean. With white Gaussian stimulation and a suitable one-filter encoding model, the result is proportional to the relevant linear filter. An even nonlinearity can make the average vanish despite strong stimulus dependence, so averaging alone is not universally sufficient.

= Stimulus-correlated spike-triggered averaging
{parent=Spike-triggered average}

For a single linear filter followed by a rate nonlinearity and a Gaussian stimulus-history vector $s$ with covariance $C$, Gaussian integration by parts gives $\mathbb E[s\,g(k^Ts)]\propto Ck$. Thus a <spike-triggered average> estimates $Ck$, rather than $k$ directly, when the stimulus is correlated. Covariance correction, with regularization if necessary, can recover the filter direction. For an even nonlinearity the proportionality factor can vanish; then a one-filter average is insufficient.

= Neural coding
{parent=Computational neuroscience}
{wiki}

How neural responses represent variables relevant to an organism. A coding claim specifies both which response features carry information and how a downstream system can use them.

= Receptive field
{parent=Neural coding}
{wiki}

The region and stimulus features whose variation influences a sensory neuron's response. A visual receptive field may include spatially distinct ON and OFF regions; a linear receptive-field kernel is a signed weighting map, while the full response can be nonlinear and depend on stimulus history.

= Temporal coding
{parent=Neural coding}

Representation by spike timing, latency, phase or relative timing across neurons, beyond a chosen count summary. Its interpretation requires a timing reference and a decoder capable of resolving the relevant precision.

= Rate coding
{parent=Neural coding}

Representation by a neuron's firing frequency or the expected spike count in a chosen time window. The window length and averaging convention matter; equal counts do not imply equal temporal patterns.

= Spike train
{parent=Neural coding}
{title2=$n(t)=\sum_i\delta(t-t_i)$}

A sequence of <action potential> times, represented mathematically as a point process or a sum of <Dirac deltas>. Its event counts, intervals and correlations summarize different aspects of neural activity.

= Time-rescaled spike-train diagnostics
{parent=Spike train}
{title2=$z_i=\int_{t_i}^{t_{i+1}}\lambda(t\mid\mathcal H_t)\,dt$}

For a correctly specified conditional rate, transformed interspike intervals have independent unit-exponential distributions, apart from observation-boundary censoring. Their transforms $1-e^{-z_i}$ should be uniform. Distributional checks and serial-correlation checks assess a fitted spike model on held-out data. A history-dependent model must include past spikes in its conditional rate; stimulus-only <Poisson spike-train likelihood fitting> cannot automatically capture refractoriness.

= Enzyme
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

An <enzyme> is a biological <catalyst>: it accelerates a <chemical reaction> and is regenerated after a successful catalytic cycle. Most <enzymes> are <proteins>, but catalytic <RNA> molecules, called <ribozymes>, also exist. Many reactions needed by a <biological cell> would proceed too slowly under its ordinary conditions without <enzymes>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/][Cooper, The Cell: the central role of enzymes as biological catalysts]{ref}.

The biological beauty is selective speed. A <biological cell> contains many possible <reactants>; its <enzymes> help determine which transformations happen on a useful timescale. In <metabolism>, one step's <reaction product> can become another step's <substrate>, so selective catalysts support an organized flow of material.

The <ribosome> is a striking biological example: the machinery that builds <proteins> has <RNA> at its catalytic centre. The complete <ribosome> contains both <RNA> and <proteins>, but its bond-forming <active site> is built from <RNA>. Structural evidence established this distinction in \a[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/][Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)]{ref}.

Selection occurs at an <active site>, the region where <substrate> binding and catalytic chemistry take place. Shape and chemical interactions favour some <substrates> over others; binding can also reshape the <enzyme>, an <induced fit>. Recognition alone is insufficient: holding a <substrate> extremely tightly need not make it react quickly. Catalysis requires a favourable route through the <transition state>, the high-barrier configuration between <reactants> and <reaction products>. Positioning reactive groups and stabilizing this configuration relative to the bound <substrate> can lower that barrier. Specificity is often strong, but need not mean exactly one acceptable <substrate>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/][Cooper, The Cell: mechanisms of enzymatic catalysis]{ref}.

\Image[enzyme-catalysis-energy.png]
{title=<Enzymes> lower reaction barriers while leaving the overall <Gibbs free energy> difference unchanged}
{description=Original illustrative profiles, with the free <enzyme> included in both endpoint states. The horizontal coordinate orders configurations along each route; it is neither elapsed time nor a measured distance. Actual catalytic mechanisms can have several intermediates and barriers.}

Read the diagram numerically. Its chosen <Gibbs free energies> are $0$ for the <reactants> and $-10\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ for the <reaction products>. The uncatalysed peak is $60\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, whereas the catalysed peak is $40\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The forward free-energy barriers are therefore $60$ and $40\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$; viewed from the <reaction products>, the reverse barriers are $70$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Both decrease by $20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, while the endpoint difference stays $-10\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. These are constructed values, not measurements of a particular <enzyme>.

This separates <chemical kinetics> from <chemical equilibrium>. An <enzyme> changes how rapidly the system approaches <chemical equilibrium>; it does not change the <equilibrium constant> for the same overall <chemical reaction> under the same conditions. Faster catalysis therefore supplies no extra driving <energy>. In a <biological cell>, continuing supply of <reactants> and removal of <reaction products> can nevertheless sustain a net flow: the surroundings keep that system away from <chemical equilibrium>. See \a[https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/kinetics/ek1t3.html][IUBMB, Symbolism and terminology in enzyme kinetics: basic definitions]{ref}.

Some <enzymes> require a <cofactor (biochemistry)>, an additional nonprotein component such as a metal <ion> or a small organic molecule. Organic helpers are often called <coenzymes>; they can assist transfers that the <protein> alone cannot readily perform. The <cofactor (biochemistry)> may remain bound or exchange with the surroundings, depending on the system. A <coenzyme> can be chemically changed in one reaction and regenerated by another: regeneration of the <enzyme> does not mean every helper is unchanged in that individual reaction. Some <enzymes> need no additional <cofactor (biochemistry)>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: small molecules associated with proteins]{ref} and \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/][Alberts et al., activated carriers and their regeneration]{ref}.

How much faster does adding <substrate> make a reaction? <Enzyme kinetics> gives a quantitative answer when a model's assumptions fit. For an illustrative reaction obeying <Michaelis-Menten kinetics>, use the <Michaelis-Menten equation> with <substrate> <concentration> $s$, limiting <reaction rate> $V_{\max}$ and <Michaelis constant> $K_M$. At $s/K_M=0.1$, substitution gives $v/V_{\max}=0.1/1.1=1/11\approx0.0909$. At $s/K_M=1$, it gives $1/2$; at $s/K_M=10$, it gives $10/11\approx0.9091$. Thus a hundredfold increase in <substrate> between the first and last examples yields only a tenfold increase in <reaction rate>.

\Image[enzyme-substrate-saturation.png]
{title=Illustrative <Michaelis-Menten kinetics>: $s/K_M=0.1,1,10$ gives $v/V_{\max}=1/11,1/2,10/11$}
{description=Original normalized curve and computed examples, with a logarithmic concentration axis to separate the three values. The dashed upper line is a limiting rate approached at high <substrate> <concentration>, not reached at a finite concentration in this model.}

For a chosen $V_{\max}=110\,\mathrm{\mu M\,min^{-1}}$, those fractions mean <reaction rates> of $10$, $55$ and $100\,\mathrm{\mu M\,min^{-1}}$. Doubling <substrate> from $10K_M$ to $20K_M$ raises the normalized rate only from $10/11$ to $20/21\approx0.9524$, a relative increase of about $4.76\%$. In this simplest model, the catalytic machinery is already nearly fully occupied. Doubling the amount of active <enzyme> instead doubles $V_{\max}$ and the predicted <reaction rates> at fixed free <substrate> <concentration>, provided the other conditions are unchanged. This distinction helps decide whether additional <substrate> or additional catalytic capacity could improve throughput.

The interpretation has limits. The simplest <Michaelis-Menten kinetics> assumes a single <substrate>, fixed active <enzyme> amount, a suitable <quasi-steady-state approximation>, and initial measurements with negligible reverse reaction from accumulated <reaction product>. Substantial depletion, interacting binding sites, or inhibition by excess <substrate> can invalidate this model. Within it, $K_M$ is the <substrate> <concentration> giving half the limiting <reaction rate>; it is not generally the binding <dissociation constant> $K_d$. See \a[https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/kinetics/ek1t3.html][IUBMB, enzyme kinetics: steady-state approximation and substrate dissociation]{ref} and \a[https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/kinetics/ek4t6.html][IUBMB, Michaelis-Menten and non-Michaelis-Menten kinetics]{ref}. Measuring activity also requires specified <temperature>, <pH> and necessary <cofactor (biochemistry)> availability: a weak signal can reflect unsuitable conditions rather than little <enzyme>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK92007/][Brooks et al., Assay Guidance Manual: basics of enzymatic assays for HTS]{ref}.

= Ribozyme
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

An <RNA> <molecule> that catalyzes a <chemical reaction>.

= Active site
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= Induced fit
{parent=Active site}

A change of an <enzyme>'s shape associated with <substrate> binding, which can help position the interacting groups for <catalysis>.

= Cofactor
{disambiguate=biochemistry}
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= Coenzyme
{parent=Cofactor (biochemistry)}

A small organic helper involved in an <enzyme>'s <catalysis>. It can be transformed in one <chemical reaction> and regenerated in another.

= Substrate
{disambiguate=biochemistry}
{parent=Enzyme}

= Substrate
{synonym}

A <reaction substrate> on which an <enzyme> acts in a <chemical reaction>.

= Biological cell
{parent=Biology}
{wiki=Cell_(biology)}

A <biological cell> is a membrane-bounded basic unit of living organization. In a cell-level experiment it can be an <experimental unit>, provided that treatment assignment and relevant interactions are handled at that level.

The shared architecture of cellular life combines a selective <cell membrane>, internal <cytoplasm>, inherited information in <DNA>, and <ribosomes> that assemble <proteins>. <Metabolism> supplies transformations of matter and <energy> needed for maintenance and growth. These components form a coupled system: a boundary retains useful <molecules>, <enzymes> organize <chemical reactions>, and inherited information supports rebuilding the machinery. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: prokaryotic cells]{ref}.

A worked structural comparison: in a typical <prokaryote>, <DNA> occupies a region without a surrounding <cell nucleus>; in a typical nucleated <eukaryote>, much of the <DNA> lies inside a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>. Both still have a <cell membrane>, <cytoplasm> and <ribosomes>. The extra boundary compartmentalizes processes; it does not create a different basic principle of living organization. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: eukaryotic cells]{ref}.

\Image[cell-compartments.png]
{title=Shared cellular architecture with different DNA compartmentalization}
{description=Original simplified schematics of a <prokaryote> and a nucleated <eukaryote>. Colors identify corresponding components; sizes and component counts are illustrative, and additional structures are omitted.}

Shared architecture describes cellular life and its developmental machinery, rather than requiring every specialized <biological cell> to retain every component. A mature mammalian <red blood cell>, for example, lacks a <cell nucleus>, although it develops from nucleated precursors. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: components of blood]{ref}. This distinction makes cellular unity compatible with striking specialization.

= Cell wall
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

= Peptidoglycan
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

<Peptidoglycan> is a mesh of linked <sugar> chains cross-linked by short chains of <amino acids> in the <cell walls> of most <bacteria>. Its absence from the <cell walls> of <archaea> helps distinguish envelope chemistry without confusing either <cell wall> with a <cell membrane>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells][OpenStax Microbiology, 3.3: cell walls and peptidoglycan]{ref}.

= Cell membrane
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

= Plasma membrane
{synonym}

The <cell membrane>, also called the <plasma membrane>, is the selective boundary separating a <biological cell>'s <cytoplasm> from its surroundings. Both <prokaryotes> and <eukaryotes> have a <cell membrane>. Where a <cell wall> is present, it is a distinct structure outside the <cell membrane>; the two names are not synonyms. This separation lets a <biological cell> maintain internal conditions while exchanging material through <membrane transport>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Cell-Membrane-Plasma-Membrane][NHGRI: cell membrane]{ref}.

The familiar architecture is a <lipid bilayer> containing <membrane proteins>. In a <phospholipid>-based <lipid bilayer>, two <membrane leaflets> face opposite ways: <hydrophilic> heads face the <water> on each side, while <hydrophobic> tails face one another in the interior. The heads are therefore exposed both outside and inside the <biological cell>. This arrangement combines a water-facing surface with a barrier to many water-soluble <solutes>; the schematic represents organization rather than an atomistic structure. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the lipid bilayer]{ref}.

\Image[cell-membrane-selective-barrier.png]
{title=Two <membrane leaflets> and selective routes through a <cell membrane>}
{description=Original <cell membrane> schematic showing <phospholipids>, <hydrophilic> heads, <hydrophobic> tails, an <ion channel>, and a <carrier protein>. Arrows illustrate possible passage, not measured rates. Shapes, sizes, spacing and numbers of <molecules> are illustrative.}

<Selective permeability> means that different substances cross at different rates under comparable conditions. Small <nonpolar> <molecules> can pass through a <lipid bilayer> by <diffusion>, while <ions> encounter a strong barrier without a suitable route. An <ion channel> supplies a selective water-filled pathway when open; a <carrier protein> binds a <solute> and changes shape to expose it to the other side. These <membrane proteins> therefore add specific routes to the barrier. An open <ion channel> permits <passive transport>, whereas a <carrier protein> can mediate either <passive transport> or <active transport>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/][Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport]{ref}.

The useful combination is retention plus controlled exchange: a completely impermeable boundary would prevent uptake, whereas unrestricted mixing would erase differences in <concentration> and <membrane potential>. The <fluid mosaic model> connects these functions to an organized but mobile <lipid bilayer> containing <proteins>.

= Selective permeability
{parent=Cell membrane}

<Selective permeability> means that a <cell membrane> permits different substances to pass at different rates under comparable conditions. Both the <lipid bilayer> and the available <membrane proteins> contribute to this selection.

= Membrane protein
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

= Membrane transport protein
{parent=Membrane protein}
{wiki}

= Carrier protein
{parent=Membrane transport protein}

A membrane <carrier protein> binds a <solute> and changes its exposed binding site from one side of a <cell membrane> to the other. Depending on its mechanism and coupling, it can mediate <passive transport> or <active transport>.

= Gap junction
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

= Ion channel
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

= Membrane transport
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

<Membrane transport> is the passage of substances across a <cell membrane>. For the small <solutes> considered here, distinguish the available route from its driving force: a <lipid bilayer>, <ion channel>, or <carrier protein> determines how passage can occur, while differences in <chemical potential> and <membrane potential> determine which direction is energetically downhill. A large driving force cannot make an unavailable route conduct. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/][Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport]{ref}.

For an ideal dilute <chemical solution> at uniform <temperature> and <pressure>, with the same reference molar <chemical potential> on both sides, the molar <electrochemical potential> change for transferring a <solute> from compartment A to B is
$$
\Delta\widetilde\mu_{\mathrm{A}\to\mathrm{B}}=RT\ln\!\left(\frac{c_{\mathrm B}}{c_{\mathrm A}}\right)+zF(V_{\mathrm B}-V_{\mathrm A}).
$$
Here $c_{\mathrm A},c_{\mathrm B}$ are <concentrations>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, $z$ is signed <ion> charge number, $F$ is the <Faraday constant>, and $V_{\mathrm B}-V_{\mathrm A}$ is the <electric potential> difference. The <natural logarithm> contains a dimensionless ratio. The electrical term vanishes for an uncharged <solute>. More generally, use <thermodynamic activities> in place of the ideal <concentration> ratio. This combines the ideal-solution <chemical potential> difference with electrical <energy> per mole; see \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E01945][IUPAC Gold Book: electrochemical potential]{ref}. For a <biological cell>, taking B inside and A outside makes the voltage term use the existing <membrane potential> convention.

\Image[membrane-transport-gradients.png]
{title=<Passive transport> and <active transport> for a stipulated <concentration> difference}
{description=Ten illustrative neutral <solute> tracers are split 8:2 between equal fixed-<volume> compartments. The lower sketches compare conceptual <free energy> profiles for <passive transport> and the <solute> part of <active transport>. Heights are unscaled and are not experimental barriers; the lower-right curve excludes the driving process, so it is not a full coupled-cycle <free energy> profile.}

= Passive transport
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Passive transport> is net movement down the transported substance's <electrochemical gradient> without coupling that movement to another source of <free energy>. <Facilitated diffusion> is <passive transport> through an <ion channel> or a passive <carrier protein>. Providing such a route can speed passage, but does not turn an uphill transfer into a downhill one. For an uncharged <solute> under the ideal conditions above, net <passive transport> runs from higher to lower <concentration>; for an <ion>, the <membrane potential> also matters. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9847/][Cooper, The Cell: transport of small molecules]{ref}.

In the figure, stipulate eight neutral <solute> tracers in A and two in B, with equal fixed <volumes>, identical surroundings, and no imposed <electric potential> difference. The <concentration> ratio is $c_{\mathrm A}/c_{\mathrm B}=8/2=4$, so A-to-B transfer has $\Delta\widetilde\mu=-RT\ln4\simeq-1.38629RT$, or $-3.458\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at the assumed <temperature> $T=300\,\mathrm K$. If this <solute> can cross, <thermodynamic equilibrium> has equal expected <concentrations>: five tracers per side on average. Individual crossings occur in both directions; an observed ten-particle snapshot need not contain exactly five on each side. These stipulated counts illustrate a driving force, not a measured flow rate or a prediction of the time to equilibrate.

For a separate illustrative <ion> calculation, keep $c_{\mathrm A}/c_{\mathrm B}=4$, but assign $z=+1$ and $V_{\mathrm A}-V_{\mathrm B}=-60\,\mathrm{mV}$ at $300\,\mathrm K$. Using $R\simeq8.31446\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $F\simeq96485.33\,\mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, B-to-A transfer gives $RT\ln4-0.060F\simeq3.458-5.789=-2.331\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Thus an appropriate open <ion channel> can permit <passive transport> toward the higher <concentration>: the electrical contribution outweighs the opposing <chemical potential> contribution. This stipulated <membrane potential> is not inferred from the tracer counts.

= Facilitated diffusion
{parent=Passive transport}
{wiki}

<Facilitated diffusion> is <passive transport> through a <membrane transport protein>, such as an <ion channel> or <carrier protein>, down the relevant <electrochemical gradient>. The <protein> supplies a route; it does not by itself provide <free energy> for uphill transfer.

= Active transport
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Active transport> moves a substance uphill in its <electrochemical gradient> by coupling the transfer to a downhill process. Some <carrier proteins> use <ATP> <hydrolysis>; others couple uphill transfer of one <solute> to downhill transfer of another <solute>, often an <ion>. The latter mechanism uses a stored <electrochemical gradient> and need not consume <ATP> directly at that <carrier protein>. Maintaining the driving <electrochemical gradient> still requires a source of <energy>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26896/][Alberts et al.: carrier proteins and active membrane transport]{ref}.

For the neutral 8:2 example, B-to-A transfer costs $RT\ln4\simeq1.38629RT$ per mole. As an explicitly assumed bookkeeping example, suppose a tightly coupled cycle supplies a downhill <free energy> change of $-2RT$ per mole of transferred <solute>. The total is then $\Delta G_{\mathrm{cycle}}=RT\ln4-2RT\simeq-0.61371RT$. This makes forward cycling energetically possible under those assumptions; it specifies neither a molecular mechanism nor a rate. Merely adding a <carrier protein> cannot supply the missing <free energy>. Conversely, as the transported <concentration> ratio rises, the required uphill cost rises, so a fixed driving process has a finite thermodynamic limit.

= Exocytosis
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Exocytosis> releases the contents of a membrane-bounded compartment to the extracellular space by fusion with the <cell membrane>. <Synaptic vesicle> fusion is an example. Its contents are released, while its membrane becomes connected to the <cell membrane>; see <synaptic vesicle> for recovery and reuse.

= Endocytosis
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Endocytosis> takes material into a <biological cell> by inward budding and separation of part of its <cell membrane>. At a <chemical synapse>, it helps recover membrane and <proteins> after <synaptic vesicle> <exocytosis>. This recovery is distinct from uptake of individual <neurotransmitter> <molecules> through <membrane transport proteins>.

= Fluid mosaic model
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

The <fluid mosaic model> describes a <cell membrane> as a fluid <lipid bilayer> with embedded <membrane proteins>. It connects a continuous barrier to a mosaic of functional components: <diffusion> within the membrane can coexist with selective passage across it. S. J. Singer and G. L. Nicolson presented the <fluid mosaic model> in Science in 1972; see \a[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4333397/][Singer and Nicolson, The fluid mosaic model of the structure of cell membranes]{ref}. The useful picture does not require every <protein> to move freely: attachments and interactions can restrict movement, as discussed in \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9898/][Cooper, The Cell: structure of the plasma membrane]{ref}.

= Cytoplasm
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

= Nucleoid
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <nucleoid> is the region occupied by <chromosome> <DNA> in a <prokaryotic> <biological cell>. It has no surrounding <nuclear envelope>, but its <DNA> is organized rather than freely spread at random. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells][OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.2: the nucleoid]{ref}.

= Organelle
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

An <organelle> is a specialized subcellular structure. Usage varies over whether non-membrane-bounded structures such as <ribosomes> count as <organelles>, so explicitly say membrane-bounded <organelles> when comparing <eukaryotic> compartmentalization with <prokaryotic> organization. <Mitochondria> and <chloroplasts> are membrane-bounded examples. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells][OpenStax Microbiology, 3.4: cellular compartments]{ref}.

= Mitochondria
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

= Mitochondrion
{synonym}

A <mitochondrion> is an <organelle> whose inner membrane houses machinery for much of aerobic <cellular respiration> and <ATP> production in typical <eukaryotic> cells. <Mitochondria> descend from a bacterial <endosymbiosis> and retain a <genome> and <ribosomes>, although many required <proteins> are encoded by nuclear <genes>. This combination connects compartmentalized function to <evolution>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins][OpenStax Biology 2e, 23.1: mitochondrial ancestry]{ref}.

= Chloroplast
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <chloroplast> is a compartment in photosynthetic <plant> and <algae> <biological cells>. Its internal <thylakoids> carry out <light-dependent reactions>; its surrounding <chloroplast stroma> contains the <enzymes> of the <Calvin cycle>. <Cyanobacteria> perform <oxygenic photosynthesis> without <chloroplasts>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: chloroplasts and photosynthesis]{ref}.

= Chloroplast stroma
{parent=Chloroplast}

The <chloroplast stroma> is the fluid compartment inside the <chloroplast> envelope but outside its <thylakoids>. The <Calvin cycle> operates here; the interior of a <thylakoid> is a separate compartment.

= Thylakoid
{parent=Chloroplast}
{wiki}

A <thylakoid> is a flattened membrane-bounded compartment. In a <chloroplast>, its membrane contains the machinery for <light-dependent reactions>, including <chlorophyll>. Stacks of <thylakoids> pack membrane area into a small space within the <chloroplast>; the <light-dependent reactions> schematic simplifies this architecture.

= Cell nucleus
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

= Nuclear envelope
{parent=Cell nucleus}
{wiki}

The <nuclear envelope> is the double-membrane boundary around a <cell nucleus>. Regulated passage connects nuclear processes to the surrounding <cytoplasm>: <transcription> produces <RNA> in the <cell nucleus>, while exported <messenger RNA> can undergo <biological translation> on <ribosomes> outside it. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nuclear-Membrane][NHGRI: nuclear membrane]{ref}.

= Ribosome
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

= Gliding bacterium
{parent=Biological cell}

A <gliding bacterium> moves along a surface without swimming through the bulk fluid. In a thin-fluid-film model, its relative motion dissipates energy through <Couette flow>, although the actuation mechanism can involve additional dissipation.

= Neutrophil
{parent=Biological cell}

A <neutrophil> is an immune <biological cell>. Its adhesive rolling on a surface involves contact mechanics, fluid stresses, and binding and unbinding of adhesive bonds.

= Red blood cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A red blood cell is a specialized <biological cell> that transports <oxygen> in blood. Its deformability lets it pass through vessels narrower than its unstressed size. A compliant-body <lubrication> model isolates the coupling between its deformation, the surrounding viscous film and a driving <pressure> difference; it is an idealization rather than a complete membrane or cytoplasm model.

= Membrane potential
{parent=Biological cell}
{title2=$V=V_{\mathrm{inside}}-V_{\mathrm{outside}}$}
{wiki}

The voltage difference across a cell membrane. Ion concentration gradients and selective membrane conductances determine it; changing conductance can change the voltage.

= Depolarization
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}

For a negative neuronal <membrane potential>, <depolarization> raises it toward zero or beyond, making the interior less negative relative to the exterior. This describes a voltage change, not necessarily increased <action potential> generation; see the depolarizing inhibition example under <synaptic potential>.

= Hyperpolarization
{parent=Membrane potential}

<Hyperpolarization> makes a neuronal <membrane potential> more negative relative to the exterior. It is a voltage description; inhibition need not produce <hyperpolarization>, as <shunting inhibition> illustrates.

= Reversal potential
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$I=g(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V)$}

= Voltage-gated ion channel
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}

A membrane channel whose opening depends on <membrane potential>. Regenerative activation and subsequent inactivation or opposing currents help produce an <action potential>.

= Two-state ion-channel gating
{parent=Voltage-gated ion channel}
{title2=$\dot X=\alpha_X(V)(1-X)-\beta_X(V)X$}

For a gate that switches independently between closed and open states, its open probability obeys the displayed balance equation. At fixed voltage, $X_\infty=\alpha_X/(\alpha_X+\beta_X)$ and $\tau_X=1/(\alpha_X+\beta_X)$. A channel requiring several independent permissive gates to be open has an open probability equal to their product. In the <Hodgkin-Huxley model>, sodium and potassium conductances are $\bar g_{\rm Na}m^3h$ and $\bar g_{\rm K}n^4$ respectively.

= Metabolism
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

The network of <chemical reactions> that maintains a living system, supplies energy and builds or processes its material. Gas emissions can arise as <primary metabolic byproducts> or <secondary metabolic byproducts>, with different implications for an <atmospheric biosignature gas>.

<Enzymes> make this network operate on biologically useful timescales. Following material through successive <substrates> and <reaction products> explains why a single fast <enzyme> need not make the entire network fast: another step may constrain the flow. Changing <enzyme> amounts or activity therefore changes which routes can carry material, connecting molecular <chemical kinetics> to the maintenance and growth of a <biological cell>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: multienzyme complexes and cell metabolism]{ref}.

= Photosynthesis
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Photosynthesis> captures <light> <energy> and transforms it into chemical forms that a <biological cell> can use. In <plants>, <algae> and <cyanobacteria>, this supports <carbon fixation>: incorporation of <carbon> from <carbon dioxide> into <chemical compounds> used to build living material. Capturing <light> and incorporating <carbon> are distinct processes coupled through <metabolism>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.1: overview of photosynthesis]{ref}.

For <oxygenic photosynthesis>, a simplified net equation writes a six-<carbon> <sugar>, <glucose>, as the representative product:
$$
6\mathrm{CO}_2+6\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}+\text{light}\longrightarrow\mathrm{C}_6\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{O}_6+6\mathrm{O}_2.
$$
This is net <stoichiometry>, not a one-step mechanism. A worked bookkeeping check counts six <carbon> <chemical atoms>, twelve <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and eighteen <oxygen> <chemical atoms> on each side: the product side has six <oxygen> <chemical atoms> in the <sugar> and twelve in <molecular oxygen>. The <light> label supplies <energy>, not additional <chemical atoms>.

Crucially, the released <molecular oxygen> comes from <water>, not <carbon dioxide>. Cancelling <water> made and consumed in different steps hides this distinction in the net equation. The <Calvin cycle> supplies a net three-<carbon> <triose phosphate>, such as <glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate>, for subsequent <sugar> synthesis; it does not directly export <glucose>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.2: light-dependent reactions]{ref} and \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: using light energy to make organic molecules]{ref}.

\Image[photosynthesis-energy-and-matter.png]
{title=<Photosynthesis>: <water> supplies released <molecular oxygen>; <carbon dioxide> supplies <sugar> <carbon>}
{description=Original schematic of <oxygenic photosynthesis>. Solid blue arrows trace selected material transfers; dashed orange arrows show <energy> transfer and the coupling through <ATP> and <NADPH>. These carriers are also <molecules>, not pure <energy>. Arrows omit intermediate <chemical reactions> and do not specify rates or yields.}

The useful achievement is connecting incoming <light> to both usable <energy> and new biological material. <ATP> is an immediate <energy> currency; <NADPH> supplies <electrons> for synthesis; <carbon dioxide> supplies the <carbon> incorporated into <carbohydrates>. These roles are complementary. <Conservation of energy> still holds: <photosynthesis> transforms <energy>, never creates it, and some absorbed <energy> is dissipated as <heat>. A <plant> also carries out <cellular respiration>, including in <biological cells> that perform <photosynthesis>. Gas exchange reflects both processes. <Oxygenic photosynthesis> remains central to interpreting terrestrial <molecular oxygen> as an <atmospheric biosignature gas>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: photosynthesis and respiration]{ref}.

= Oxygenic photosynthesis
{parent=Photosynthesis}

<Oxygenic photosynthesis> uses <water> as an <electron> source and releases <molecular oxygen>. <Plants>, <algae> and <cyanobacteria> provide examples. The defining distinction from <anoxygenic photosynthesis> is <molecular oxygen> release, not merely capture of <light>.

= Anoxygenic photosynthesis
{parent=Photosynthesis}
{wiki}

<Anoxygenic photosynthesis> captures <light> without producing <molecular oxygen>. It occurs in some <bacteria> and uses <electron> sources other than <water>. Whether <carbon fixation> accompanies this <light> capture depends on the <organism> and conditions; <photosynthesis> does not imply one universal <carbon>-assimilation pathway. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/][Alberts et al.: chloroplasts and photosynthetic bacteria]{ref}.

= Light-dependent reactions
{parent=Photosynthesis}
{wiki}

In a <chloroplast>, <light-dependent reactions> take place at <thylakoid> membranes. <Chlorophyll> captures <light>; <electron> transfer helps establish an <electrochemical gradient> across the <thylakoid> membrane, enabling formation of <ATP>. The <oxygenic photosynthesis> pathway also forms <NADPH> and releases <molecular oxygen> from <water>. <ATP> and <NADPH> become available in the <chloroplast stroma> for the <Calvin cycle>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/][Alberts et al.: chloroplast light reactions]{ref}.

\Image[chloroplast-photosynthesis-compartments.png]
{title=<Thylakoid> membranes and <chloroplast stroma> couple two stages of <photosynthesis>}
{description=Original <chloroplast> schematic, not an experimental photograph or a scale drawing. <Light-dependent reactions> supply <ATP> and <NADPH> to the stromal <Calvin cycle>; depleted carriers return for reuse. The <molecular oxygen> arrow leaves the <water>-using stage, while <carbon dioxide> enters <carbon fixation>.}

= Chlorophyll
{parent=Light-dependent reactions}
{wiki}

<Chlorophyll> is a family of <molecules> that absorb <light> for <photosynthesis>. In <chloroplasts>, <chlorophyll> works with <proteins> in <thylakoid> membranes; absorbing <light> initiates <energy> capture rather than directly constructing a <sugar>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.2: photosynthetic pigments]{ref}.

= Carbon fixation
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Carbon fixation> incorporates <carbon> from <carbon dioxide> into organic <chemical compounds>. It identifies a material transformation, rather than a particular source of <energy>: <carbon fixation> can be supported by <photosynthesis> or by other <metabolism>. The <Calvin cycle> is one pathway, not the definition of all <carbon fixation>.

= Calvin cycle
{c}
{parent=Carbon fixation}
{wiki}

= Calvin-Benson cycle
{c}
{synonym}

The <Calvin cycle> couples <carbon fixation> to use of <ATP> and <NADPH>. In a <chloroplast> it takes place in the <chloroplast stroma>. Its <chemical reactions> incorporate <carbon dioxide>, use <NADPH>-supplied <electrons>, and regenerate the <molecules> that accept further <carbon dioxide>.

In the standard <Calvin cycle> bookkeeping, three <carbon dioxide> <molecules> require nine <ATP> and six <NADPH> for one net three-<carbon> <triose phosphate>. A six-<carbon> <sugar> equivalent therefore corresponds to six <carbon dioxide>, eighteen <ATP> and twelve <NADPH>; subsequent <sugar> synthesis is separate. These are pathway requirements, not a universal measured <photosynthesis> yield. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: Calvin cycle bookkeeping]{ref}.

Calling the <Calvin cycle> light-independent means its individual <chemical reactions> do not directly capture <light>; it does not mean the <Calvin cycle> runs only at night or independently of the supplies from <light-dependent reactions>.

= Cellular respiration
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Cellular respiration> transfers <energy> from chemical fuels into usable forms including <ATP>. <Plants> carry out <cellular respiration> as well as <photosynthesis>; in its aerobic form, <cellular respiration> consumes <molecular oxygen> and releases <carbon dioxide>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules][OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: energy flow]{ref}.

= Secondary metabolic byproduct
{parent=Metabolism}

A compound released through specialized functions such as signaling, defense or stress adaptation rather than the core reactions needed for energy and biomass. <Dimethyl sulfide> and <chloromethane> are examples discussed as atmospheric biosignatures. Their specialized origins may improve specificity while lowering the global emission flux.

= Primary metabolic byproduct
{parent=Metabolism}

A product released by reactions needed for energy acquisition, growth or biomass construction. <Methane> from energy-yielding microbial reactions and <molecular oxygen> from oxygenic <photosynthesis> are examples. Abiotic chemistry can make some of the same molecules, so production mechanism and environmental abundance matter.

= Evolution
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

<Evolution> is descent with inherited modification. Within a population, changes in <allele frequencies> provide a precise genetic description; over longer histories, branching descent connects populations through <common descent>. An individual growing larger is <biological development>, whereas an inherited variant becoming more frequent across generations is <evolution>. See \a[https://evolution.berkeley.edu/evolution-101/an-introduction-to-evolution/][UC Museum of Paleontology: the definition of evolution]{ref}.

<Mutation> supplies new <alleles>, <genetic recombination> reshuffles inherited material, <natural selection> favors variants with greater reproductive contribution in a specified environment, and <genetic drift> changes frequencies through chance. <Gene flow> moves variants between populations. Consequently, an increasing <allele frequency> alone does not identify <natural selection>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-2-population-genetics][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: population genetics]{ref}.

Evidence includes ordered <fossils>, shared inherited structures and similarities in <DNA>; <phylogenetics> uses such observations to infer relationships. A <phylogenetic tree> is an inferred historical account, with uncertainty about particular branches. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/18-1-understanding-evolution][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: evidence of evolution]{ref}. Evidence also comes from experiments: Blount, Borland and Lenski reported an <Escherichia coli> lineage able to use <citrate> with <oxygen> present by 31,500 generations, and tested historical dependence by replaying <evolution> from stored ancestors. See \a[https://www.blount-lab.org/uploads/2/3/0/3/23037222/2008_pnas_blount_et_al.pdf][Blount, Borland and Lenski, 2008: original experimental report]{ref}. Such observations constrain explanations; no single frequency equation captures every evolutionary process.

= Common descent
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

= Gene flow
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

= Natural selection
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

<Natural selection> involves systematic differences in expected <reproductive success> associated with variation in <phenotype>. For a continuing evolutionary response, the relevant variation must be inherited: offspring must tend to preserve the differences being favored. Actual offspring counts also fluctuate by chance, producing <genetic drift>. Survival matters insofar as it affects contribution to future generations. <Genotype fitness> is therefore contextual, depending on environment and the life cycle being counted, rather than simply measuring strength or longevity. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-1-population-evolution][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: population evolution]{ref}.

In chapter IV of his 1859 book, Darwin explained <natural selection> through preservation of favorable variations and greater chances of survival and reproduction. See \a[https://darwin-online.org.uk/converted/published/1859_Origin_F373/1859_Origin_F373.html][Darwin, On the Origin of Species, first edition, chapter IV]{ref}. The beautiful consequence is cumulative change without foresight: inherited differences are filtered repeatedly by present conditions. <Natural selection> neither generates a needed <mutation> on demand nor guarantees progress toward a predetermined goal.

= Haploid selection recurrence
{parent=Natural selection}
{title2=$p'=\frac{wp}{wp+(1-p)}$}

Consider an explicitly idealized <haploid> population with two inherited types $A,B$, one <allele> per individual at the tracked <genetic locus>, and discrete, nonoverlapping generations. Assume faithful type transmission, no <mutation> or <gene flow>, a constant environment, and reproductive contributions independent of type frequency. Let the <expected values> of offspring surviving to the next census be $m_A,m_B>0$, with relative <genotype fitness> $w=m_A/m_B$.

For $A$ frequency $p$, expected offspring contributions are proportional to $pm_A$ and $(1-p)m_B$. Normalizing gives the deterministic large-population <mathematical model>
$$
p'=\frac{pm_A}{pm_A+(1-p)m_B}=\frac{wp}{wp+(1-p)}.
$$
The denominator is mean relative <genotype fitness>. For $w=1.2$ and $p_0=0.2$, $p_1=0.24/1.04=3/13\approx0.23077$. Equivalently, 200 $A$ and 800 $B$ parents with $m_A=1.2,m_B=1$ contribute expected counts 240 and 800. Fractional per-parent <expected values> are averages, not literal fractional offspring. The ratio of these expectations is a deterministic approximation, not generally the expectation of a random ratio.

The odds multiply particularly simply:
$$
\frac{p_{t+1}}{1-p_{t+1}}=w\frac{p_t}{1-p_t},\qquad p_t=\frac{w^tp_0}{1-p_0+w^tp_0}.
$$
Thus $p_{10}\approx0.60753$: repeated modest advantage can substantially change composition. For $w>1$ and $0<p<1$, $p'-p=(w-1)p(1-p)/(1+(w-1)p)>0$. The deterministic boundary $p=0$ remains unchanged: <natural selection> needs existing variation.

\Image[natural-selection-frequency.png]
{title=Deterministic selection and stochastic sampling in a finite population}
{description=Both panels start at $p_0=0.2$. The left compares the deterministic <haploid selection recurrence> with eight simulated finite-population paths at $w=1.2$; the right shows eight neutral <genetic drift> paths at $w=1$. Each simulation samples $N=50$ offspring independently per generation. These are illustrative <mathematical models>, not experimental measurements.}

A distinct stochastic extension fixes $N$ offspring and samples their types independently using a <binomial distribution>: $K\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(N,q)$, where $q=wp/(wp+1-p)$, then $p'=K/N$. Its conditional <expected value> is $q$ and conditional <variance> is $q(1-q)/N$. With $w=1$, the conditional <expected value> remains $p$, yet realized frequencies wander through <genetic drift>. With $w>1$, <genetic drift> can still lower $p$ or lose $A$ entirely. Over several generations, the deterministic curve need not equal the mean stochastic trajectory, because the recurrence is nonlinear.

= Reproductive success
{parent=Natural selection}
{wiki}

= Phylogenetics
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

= Clade
{parent=Phylogenetics}
{wiki}

A <clade> comprises an ancestor and all its descendants. Membership is about <common descent>, not a taxonomic rank or a degree of cellular complexity. In a <two-domain system>, the nuclear lineage of <eukaryotes> nests within <archaea>; a group containing only the <prokaryotic> members excludes descendants and is therefore not that complete <clade>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/20-2-determining-evolutionary-relationships][OpenStax Biology 2e, 20.2: clades and shared ancestry]{ref}.

= Phylogenetic tree
{parent=Phylogenetics}
{wiki}

A <phylogenetic tree> describes ancestral relationships. In a <probabilistic graphical model> on a rooted <tree>, <latent variables> at ancestral vertices are inferred from observations at leaves using <belief propagation>.

= Felsenstein pruning algorithm
{c}
{parent=Phylogenetic tree}

The <Felsenstein pruning algorithm> computes a site <likelihood function> by <sum-product belief propagation> from leaves to root. For a finite-state <Markov kernel> $K$, the subtree <likelihood function> obeys $L_v(a)=\prod_{w\text{ child of }v}\sum_bK(a,b)L_w(b)$, with observed-state indicators at leaves. Summing against the root <probability distribution> gives the site <likelihood function>. An outward pass gives edge <posterior probabilities> and expected transition counts for the <expectation-maximization algorithm>.

= Calcium in biology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Calcium has roles in cell signalling and skeletal structure. Dietary <calcium intake> and <serum calcium> are distinct quantities: physiological regulation means a change in one need not correspond to an equivalent change in the other.

= Serum calcium
{parent=Calcium in biology}

Serum calcium is the concentration of calcium in blood serum. It can be an <exposure> in an <observational study> or <Mendelian randomization>, but genetically changing it need not reproduce the effect of changing dietary <calcium intake>.

= Hypercalcaemia
{parent=Serum calcium}
{wiki}

= Hypercalcemia
{synonym}

Hypercalcaemia is an abnormally elevated <serum calcium> concentration. A diagnosis in medical records depends on testing and diagnostic practice, which can introduce <selection bias> into an <observational study>.

= Calcium intake
{parent=Calcium in biology}

Calcium intake is the amount of calcium consumed, including food and supplements. It is an <exposure> that can be changed by an intervention, and should not be identified automatically with <serum calcium> in a <causal inference> analysis.

= Reproduction
{parent=Biology}

= Sexual reproduction
{parent=Reproduction}

= Gamete
{parent=Sexual reproduction}

A gamete is a reproductive <biological cell> that can fuse with another <gamete> during <fertilization>. In the ordinary <diploid> inheritance model, a <haploid> <gamete> carries one copy at each <genetic locus>.

= Fertilization
{parent=Sexual reproduction}

<Fertilization> is fusion of <gametes>. Fusion of two <haploid> <gametes> restores the <diploid> state in the inheritance model used for <Mendelian segregation>.

= Genetics
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

<Genetics> studies how <genes> are inherited, how their <alleles> vary, and how that variation contributes to <phenotypes>. Its central distinction is between an inherited description, the <genotype>, and what an <organism> actually expresses, the <phenotype>. Following <alleles> through a <pedigree> answers a family-level question; following <allele frequencies> through generations leads to <population genetics> and <evolution>.

The beauty of <Mendelian segregation> is that discrete inherited copies can generate predictable statistical patterns without making every offspring identical. A single <genetic locus> already connects <meiosis>, <probability> and observable variation. The <Hardy-Weinberg principle> then supplies a population baseline: it separates reshuffling inherited copies from processes such as <natural selection> that change their frequencies. These simple models are useful precisely because their assumptions can be stated and checked.

= Molecular genetics
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= Genome
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <genome> is the complete set of genetic information under discussion. For a cellular <organism> it is encoded in <DNA>, including <genes> and other sequences. Specify whether a comparison concerns a nuclear <genome>, a <mitochondrion>'s <genome> or all cellular genetic material: the <cell nucleus> does not contain every <DNA> sequence in a typical <eukaryote>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Genome][NHGRI: genome]{ref}.

= DNA replication
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<DNA replication> copies a double-stranded <DNA> <molecule> into two daughter double-stranded <DNA> <molecules>. In <semiconservative replication>, each daughter retains one parental strand and receives one newly synthesized complementary strand. <DNA polymerases> build the new strands from incoming <nucleotides>; they do not make <RNA> or <proteins> in this copying step. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): DNA replication mechanisms]{ref}.

For a five-<base pair> example, separate $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGCC}\text{-}3'$ from its partner $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$. <Base pairing> specifies a new TACGG partner for the old ATGCC strand and a new ATGCC partner for the old TACGG strand. With error-free copying, both daughter <DNA> <molecules> have the original <nucleotide sequence>, but different physical strand histories. One initial duplex contains ten <nucleotides>; one complete round produces two duplexes containing twenty <nucleotides>, ten retained and ten newly incorporated.

\Image[dna-replication-semi-conservative.png]
{title=<Semiconservative replication>: follow two original <DNA> strands through two complete copying rounds}
{description=Blue O1 and purple O2 are the original strands; green strands were made in round 1 and orange strands in round 2. Each arrow connects a parent duplex to its daughters. After round 2, two of four duplexes retain an original strand, and the other two retain a round-1 strand. All eight strands have the appropriate complementary <nucleotide sequences>; colors identify strand age, not different <nucleobases>. The flattened duplexes show ancestry and orientation, not a replication fork or molecular shape.}

This is useful for inheritance: both daughter <biological cells> can receive a copy of the <DNA> record. The elegant feature is that <base pairing> lets each strand specify its missing partner. Direction matters too: a replicating <DNA polymerase> reads its template $3'\to5'$ and extends the new strand at its $3'$ end, so synthesis proceeds $5'\to3'$. These are opposite directions on <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>, not two alternative directions of synthesis. The enzyme needs a pre-existing paired strand end to extend; the diagram omits the machinery that starts and coordinates copying. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/][Alberts et al.: template-directed synthesis and the requirement for a primer]{ref}.

The strand accounting predicts observable differences between copying mechanisms. In 1958, Meselson and Stahl grew <Escherichia coli> with the heavier <nitrogen> <isotope> $^{15}\mathrm{N}$, then supplied $^{14}\mathrm{N}$ and measured extracted <DNA> by density-gradient centrifugation. After one generation their samples contained intermediate-density <DNA>; after two, intermediate-density and light <DNA> occurred in equal amounts. This supported <semiconservative replication> in the tested <Escherichia coli> culture. It was an <isotope> assay of sampled <DNA>, not simultaneous testing of every kind of <DNA> or direct colored-strand photography. See \a[https://dosequis.colorado.edu/Courses/MethodsLogic/papers/Messelson1958.pdf][Meselson and Stahl, The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli, PNAS 44, 671–682 (1958), especially Figures 4–6; doi:10.1073/pnas.44.7.671]{ref}.

= Semiconservative replication
{parent=DNA replication}
{wiki}

In <semiconservative replication>, each daughter duplex retains one strand of its immediate parent. Under complete copying without strand loss or exchange, one starting duplex gives $2^n$ duplexes after $n$ rounds, but still only two original strands. For $n\geq1$, the fraction of duplexes containing an original strand is $2/2^n=2^{1-n}$: all after one round, half after two, a quarter after three. The fraction of all strands that are original is instead $2^{-n}$. A strand made in round 1 is parental when round 2 begins.

= DNA polymerase
{c}
{parent=DNA replication}
{wiki}

A replicating <DNA polymerase> is an <enzyme> that extends a paired strand end with <nucleotides> selected against a <DNA> template. New <DNA> grows $5'\to3'$ while the template is read $3'\to5'$. <Base pairing> specifies the partner; the <DNA polymerase> catalyzes its incorporation. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/][Alberts et al.: DNA polymerase]{ref}.

= Gene expression
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<Gene expression> uses a <gene>'s information to produce functional <RNA> or a <protein>. <Transcription> makes <RNA> from a <DNA> template; for a <protein>-coding <gene>, <biological translation> then uses <messenger RNA> to specify an <amino acid> chain. Many <genes> instead produce functional <RNA>, including <transfer RNA> and <ribosomal RNA>, without a translated <protein> product. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/][Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): from DNA to RNA]{ref}.

Follow the chosen <coding strand> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGGCTTAA}\text{-}3'$: <transcription> of its complementary template gives $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGGCUUAA}\text{-}3'$ in <messenger RNA>. In the stated <reading frame>, its three <codons> are AUG, GCU and UAA: <methionine>, <alanine> and a <stop codon>. Thus nine <nucleotides> specify two <amino acid> residues followed by termination, not three residues. This is an illustrative coding segment embedded in suitable expression and initiation context, not a claim that an isolated nine-<nucleotide> <RNA> is sufficient to initiate <biological translation>.

\Image[gene-expression-transcription-translation.png]
{title=<Gene expression>: oriented <DNA> template, <messenger RNA> <codons> and the resulting <amino acid> order}
{description=The <coding strand> matches the displayed <messenger RNA> after T is replaced by U; <RNA polymerase> uses the opposite, complementary <DNA> strand. The <ribosome> reads AUG, GCU and UAA from $5'$ to $3'$. AUG and GCU specify <methionine> and <alanine>; UAA signals termination without supplying an <amino acid>. The chain is shown from its <N-terminus> to its <C-terminus>. The labels summarize successive operations, not simultaneous binding of all depicted components; flanking signals and machinery are omitted.}

The distinction between copying and use explains how <biological cells> can preserve the same <DNA> yet produce different products. <DNA replication> duplicates the record; <gene expression> selects and uses parts of it. <Transcription> does not use all <DNA> at once, and <gene expression> can be controlled at several steps, including <transcription>, <RNA> processing and <biological translation>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-1-regulation-of-gene-expression][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: regulation of gene expression]{ref}. The arrows describe template-directed synthesis: the <DNA> does not physically turn into a <protein>, and the <messenger RNA> does not supply the <amino acids> from which that <protein> is assembled.

= Transcription
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Transcription
{synonym}

<Transcription> is synthesis of <RNA> using one <DNA> strand as a template by an <RNA polymerase>. During elongation the <RNA polymerase> reads that template $3'\to5'$ while extending <RNA> $5'\to3'$. Only the selected region is transcribed; other regions need not be active, and the template strand can differ between <genes>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/][Alberts et al.: RNA polymerase and selection of transcribed DNA regions]{ref}.

For the displayed example, align these three <nucleotide sequences> in the same left-to-right positions:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{coding DNA:}\quad&5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGGCTTAA}\text{-}3',\\
\text{template DNA:}\quad&3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACCGAATT}\text{-}5',\\
\text{RNA product:}\quad&5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGGCUUAA}\text{-}3'.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first template triplet TAC selects AUG: T pairs with A, A with U, and C with G. The next CGA selects GCU, and ATT selects UAA. The <coding strand> therefore matches the <RNA> lettering except for T versus U, whereas the template is complementary. Reading that template in its own $5'\to3'$ direction gives $5'\text{-}\mathrm{TTAAGCCAT}\text{-}3'$; orientation cannot be discarded. The <gene expression> diagram keeps all three rows aligned. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-2-prokaryotic-transcription][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: template and coding strands in transcription]{ref}.

This correspondence makes <nucleotide sequences> interpretable, but replacing T by U is valid only after identifying the <coding strand>. Moreover, <RNA> may be processed after <transcription>, so the initial product and mature <messenger RNA> need not have identical uninterrupted <nucleotide sequences>. <Transcription> also makes functional <RNA> that will not undergo <biological translation>. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/][Alberts et al.: RNA processing and RNA as the final gene product]{ref}.

= RNA polymerase
{c}
{parent=Transcription (biology)}
{wiki}

An <RNA polymerase> used in <transcription> is an <enzyme> that synthesizes <RNA> from a <DNA> template. Unlike a replicating <DNA polymerase>, it can start a new strand without a pre-existing paired strand end; initiation still requires the appropriate <DNA> signals and associated machinery. See \a[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/][Alberts et al.: RNA polymerase and transcription initiation]{ref}.

= Coding strand
{parent=Transcription (biology)}
{wiki}

For a specified <transcription> unit, the <coding strand> is the <DNA> strand matching the initial <RNA> product in the $5'\to3'$ direction, with T in place of U. The opposite <DNA> strand is the template. The name does not imply that every transcribed <gene> encodes a <protein>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-2-prokaryotic-transcription][Clark, Douglas and Choi: coding and template strands]{ref}.

= Translation
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Biological translation
{synonym}

<Biological translation> uses a <ribosome> to read <messenger RNA> <codons> $5'\to3'$ and assemble the corresponding <amino acid> chain. <Transfer RNA> adaptors match <codons> through <base pairing> and carry the <amino acids>; the <genetic code> supplies the correspondence. The chain grows from the <N-terminus> toward the <C-terminus>, by adding each new residue at the <C-terminus>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: ribosomes, transfer RNA and protein synthesis]{ref}.

In the <gene expression> diagram, read $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUG\ GCU\ UAA}\text{-}3'$ in the chosen <reading frame>. Under the standard <genetic code>, AUG specifies <methionine> and GCU specifies <alanine>; UAA is a <stop codon>. The result before any subsequent processing is the two-residue chain N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C, followed by termination. The <stop codon> contributes zero <amino acids>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code][Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: the standard genetic code]{ref}. This example assumes appropriate initiation at the displayed AUG in a suitable context in a <eukaryote>; the diagram displays the coding segment, not a complete independently translatable <messenger RNA>.

Initiation establishes the starting position and <reading frame>, using a <ribosome>, an initiating <transfer RNA> and additional factors. At a standard <stop codon>, a release factor promotes release of the chain rather than delivery of another <amino acid> by <transfer RNA>. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis][Clark, Douglas and Choi: initiation and termination of translation]{ref}. A short two-residue example illustrates decoding, not the size or folding of a typical functional <protein>.

The useful connection is between two different alphabets: <nucleotides> specify the order of <amino acids> through the <genetic code>. <Base pairing> helps select <transfer RNA>, while the <ribosome> catalyzes joining the delivered <amino acids>. The <messenger RNA> remains a template rather than being consumed as the chain's building material. Separating template, adaptor and product makes clear why <transcription> and <biological translation> are distinct mechanisms.

= Genetic code
{parent=Translation (biology)}
{wiki}

The <genetic code> assigns <RNA> <codons> to <amino acids> or termination signals. Four possible <nucleobases> at each of three positions give $4^3=64$ <codons>. In the standard <genetic code>, 61 specify <amino acids> and three are <stop codons>; several <codons> can specify the same <amino acid>. Variants exist, so state which <genetic code> is being used. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code][Clark, Douglas and Choi: genetic code assignments and variants]{ref}.

= Codon
{parent=Genetic code}

A <codon> is a three-<nucleotide> unit read in a specified <reading frame> during <biological translation>. For example, GCU specifies <alanine> in the standard <genetic code>. Its letters are read $5'\to3'$.

= Stop codon
{parent=Codon}
{wiki}

In the standard <genetic code>, UAA, UAG and UGA are <stop codons>: they signal termination of <biological translation> rather than an <amino acid> assignment. See \a[https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code][Clark, Douglas and Choi: stop codons]{ref}.

= Reading frame
{parent=Genetic code}
{wiki}

A <reading frame> specifies how consecutive <nucleotides> are grouped into <codons>. Starting at position 1 of AUGGCUUAA gives AUG / GCU / UAA; starting at position 2 gives UGG / CUU with AA left over. The letters are unchanged but the groups differ. A possible grouping alone does not establish an actual <biological translation> initiation site.

= Non-coding DNA
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

= Ploidy
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$n,2n,\ldots$}

= Haploid
{parent=Ploidy}
{title2=$n$}

= Diploid
{parent=Ploidy}
{title2=$2n$}

Diploid <biological cells> have two sets of <chromosomes>. In the ordinary one-locus inheritance model, a <diploid> <genotype> therefore contains two <allele> copies at each <genetic locus>.

= Panmixia
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= Random mating
{synonym}

Panmixia is random mating within a breeding population, without systematic choice by genotype or phenotype. In a homogeneous genetic model it makes the two parental gamete draws independent, yielding the <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium> genotype probabilities $p_i^2$ and $2p_ip_j$. It is a modelling assumption; <assortative mating> and <population stratification> are distinct ways a population can depart from it.

// Target: causal-inference.bigb

= Mutation
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A mutation changes inherited genetic sequence. Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, every mutation occurs at a previously unmutated position, so mutations below the sample's common ancestor correspond to <segregating sites>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Mutation rate
{parent=Mutation}
{title2=$u$}
{wiki}

A mutation rate measures the frequency of <mutation> events per specified unit, such as per chromosome segment per generation. Its units and the population-size convention must be specified. If time is measured in $N$ generations and $\theta=2Nu$, each ancestral lineage has mutation intensity $Nu=\theta/2$ per unit of coalescent time.

= Chromosome
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A chromosome carries <genes> and other genetic material. A <haplotype> records the <alleles> carried on one chromosome at specified <genetic loci>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Gene
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <gene> is an inherited functional unit of genetic material. In cellular <organisms>, a <gene> is a region of <DNA> whose information produces a functional <RNA> or contributes to a <protein>. This connects inheritance to cellular chemistry: a change in a <gene> can alter the amount or activity of a <protein>, and thereby a <phenotype>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Gene][NHGRI: gene]{ref}.

Alternative inherited versions at a <genetic locus> are <alleles>. The distinction matters: two parents can carry the same <gene> but different <alleles>, and a <genetic locus> can have variation outside a <gene>. Neither the word <gene> nor a change in <DNA> alone specifies the resulting <phenotype>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Genetic-study ascertainment
{parent=Genetics}

= Ascertainment in a genetic study
{synonym}

<Ascertainment in a genetic study> specifies how individuals or families enter a genetic study. If $A$ is the event of inclusion, then $P(X\mid A)=P(X)P(A\mid X)/P(A)$. Selection on disease <phenotype> can change an unconditional <pedigree likelihood>. When comparing <genetic marker> <probabilities> conditional on a specified disease pattern, selection depending only on that pattern cancels from the linkage <likelihood ratio>.

= Meiosis
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= Meioses
{synonym}

<Meiosis> is the chromosome-reduction process producing gametes from diploid cells. <Mendelian segregation> chooses between parental copies, while <genetic recombination> can produce new combinations at multiple <genetic loci>.

= Mendelian segregation
{parent=Genetics}
{c}
{title2=$Aa\times Aa:\quad\tfrac14AA,\ \tfrac12Aa,\ \tfrac14aa$}

In ordinary <diploid> <meiosis>, the two copies at a <genetic locus> separate: each <gamete> receives one copy. With fair transmission, a <heterozygote> $Aa$ produces <gametes> carrying $A$ or $a$ with <probability> $1/2$ each. This is a transmission mechanism conditional on a parental <genotype>, rather than a statement about how common either <allele> is in a population.

For the one-locus cross $Aa\times Aa$, assume independent parental transmissions, no new <mutation>, and no differences in <fertilization> or survival between the resulting <genotypes>. Multiplying the two <gamete> <probabilities> gives four equally likely ordered outcomes: $AA,Aa,aA,aa$, each with <probability> $1/4$. Combining the two orders of the <heterozygote> gives
$$
P(AA)=0.25,\qquad P(Aa)=0.50,\qquad P(aa)=0.25.
$$
Only under the additional ideal model of <complete dominance> of $A$, complete <penetrance>, and no environmental changes to the scored characteristic do $AA$ and $Aa$ share the dominant <phenotype>. Its <probability> is then $0.25+0.50=0.75$. The inheritance calculation alone does not determine a <phenotype> or predict a generic <disease> risk.

\Image[mendelian-segregation.png]
{title=Fair Mendelian transmission in an Aa × Aa cross, with phenotype grouping under complete dominance and penetrance}

These are <probabilities>, not a requirement that every four offspring contain one $AA$, two $Aa$ and one $aa$. For 100 independent offspring under the ideal model, the <expected values> of the three counts are $25,50,25$; the $aa$ count has a <binomial distribution> with <standard deviation> $\sqrt{100(0.25)(0.75)}\approx4.33$. Each new transmission keeps the same <conditional probabilities> given the parental <genotypes>.

In his paper published in 1866, Mendel reported 5,474 round and 1,850 wrinkled pea seeds: $5474/7324\approx0.7474$ round, close to the ideal $0.75$. Following descendants also distinguished two inherited classes within the round <phenotype>, illustrating why <genotype> cannot always be read from appearance. See \a[https://cs.brown.edu/people/rblumber/MendelWeb/Mendel.html][Mendel, Experiments in Plant Hybridization, English translation, sections 5–6]{ref}. At several <genetic loci>, transmission patterns additionally depend on <genetic linkage> and <genetic recombination>.

= Independent assortment
{parent=Mendelian segregation}
{title2=$\theta=\tfrac12$}

<Independent assortment> between two diallelic <genetic loci> means that the transmitted <allele> at one <genetic locus> is independent of that at the other, conditional on the parental states. In the ordinary two-locus linkage model this has <recombination fraction> $1/2$, with each of four combinations equally likely for a parent <heterozygous> at both <genetic loci>.

= Genetic marker
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <genetic marker> is an observed inherited genomic state used to track chromosomes, test <genetic association>, or perform <genetic linkage> analysis. <Genetic marker> <alleles> need not themselves cause the <phenotype>. Several <genetic markers> on one nonrecombining region form a single observed <haplotype>.

= Identity by descent
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= IBD
{synonym}
{c}

= Identical by descent
{synonym}

Allele copies are identical by descent if they derive from the same specified ancestral copy. Identical observed states need not imply this ancestry. Two diploid individuals are 2-IBD when both of their allele copies can be paired as identical by descent; autozygous individuals carrying two copies of the same ancestral copy are mutually 2-IBD when that ancestor is shared.

= Sibling IBD ascertainment under multiplicative penetrance
{parent=Identity by descent}
{title2=$(z_0,z_1,z_2)=\dfrac{(\alpha^4,2\alpha^2\beta,\beta^2)}{(\alpha^2+\beta)^2}$}

For <full siblings> in an <outbred pedigree>, condition on both being affected under <multiplicative diallelic penetrance> and on residual disease outcomes being independent given their <genotypes>. The usual <Mendelian IBD sharing of full siblings> probabilities $(1/4,1/2,1/4)$ are weighted by $p^2\alpha^4,p^2\alpha^2\beta,p^2\beta^2$, respectively, giving the displayed conditional sharing probabilities. Since $\beta-\alpha^2=\phi(1-\phi)(\theta-1)^2\geq0$, $z_1\leq1/2$, strictly for a polymorphic locus with a nontrivial effect.

= Jacquard identity coefficients
{parent=Identity by descent}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta_1,\ldots,\Delta_9$}

For two individuals carrying labelled copies $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$, condensed identity coefficients give the <probabilities> of nine <identity by descent> patterns, identifying states that differ only by exchanging the two homologues within either individual. They retain within-individual <autozygosity>, which three ordinary outbred sharing coefficients omit. One convention is
$$
\begin{array}{c|l}
1&abcd\\2&ab\mid cd\\3&abc\mid d\text{ or }abd\mid c\\4&ab\mid c\mid d\\5&acd\mid b\text{ or }bcd\mid a\\6&cd\mid a\mid b\\7&ac\mid bd\text{ or }ad\mid bc\\8&\text{one cross-individual pair, two singletons}\\9&a\mid b\mid c\mid d
\end{array}
$$
Each block consists of copies identical by descent, and different blocks have distinct ancestral origins. For siblings whose parents are otherwise outbred <first cousins>, these coefficients are $(1,0,2,1,2,1,15,30,12)/64$. This follows by conditioning on whether the parents share one ancestral copy and enumerating the four independent <Mendelian segregation> choices to their two children. The <kinship coefficient> is $\Delta_1+(\Delta_3+\Delta_5+\Delta_7)/2+\Delta_8/4$, here $9/32$. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2585748/[Weir's discussion of identity states] uses this convention.

= Identity-by-descent sharing of inbred full siblings
{parent=Jacquard identity coefficients}
{title2=$(P(J=0),P(J=1),P(J=2))=(7/32,17/32,1/4)$}

Let $J$ be the largest number of chromosome copies that can be paired across the two <full siblings> as <identical by descent>, counting repeated copies when a child is <autozygous>. For offspring of otherwise outbred <first cousins>, with probability $3/4$ the parental copies have no common ancestral origin, giving ordinary sibling sharing $(1/4,1/2,1/4)$. With probability $1/4$, write the parental copies as $(A,B)$ and $(A,C)$ with three distinct origins. Each child independently has one of $AA,AB,AC,BC$, equally likely. Among sixteen ordered child pairs, two share no copy, ten share one, and four share two. The mixture gives the displayed probabilities. These three numbers do not determine all <Jacquard identity coefficients>; the formula $\phi=\mathbb E[J]/4$ is restricted to noninbred individuals and fails here.

= First-cousin identity-by-descent probabilities
{parent=Identity by descent}
{title2=$(k_0,k_1,k_2)=(3/4,1/4,0)$}

For <first cousins> in an otherwise <outbred pedigree>, compare the copies transmitted from their related parents, who are <full siblings>. These transmissions trace to the same shared grandparent with <probability> $1/2$ and then to the same homologous copy of that grandparent with probability $1/2$, giving $k_1=1/4$. The unrelated parental contributions cannot match, so two shared copies have probability zero. Selecting one random copy from each cousin chooses those two potentially shared copies with probability $1/4$, giving <kinship coefficient> $k_1/4=1/16$.

= Identical-by-descent segment
{parent=Identity by descent}

= IBD segment
{synonym}
{c}

An identical-by-descent segment is a chromosomal tract inherited from the same ancestral chromosome without an intervening breakpoint in the tracked ancestry. With a specified total number $m$ of independent <meioses> and a uniform recombination map, the probability that a fixed tract of genetic length $d$ survives without <genetic recombination> is approximately $e^{-md}$. Recent common ancestry therefore tends to give longer shared tracts. Lengths of tracts selected by covering a random point are length-biased relative to tracts sampled by their boundaries.

= Kinship coefficient
{parent=Identity by descent}
{title2=$\phi_{XY}$}

The kinship coefficient $\phi_{XY}$ is the <probability> that one randomly selected <allele> copy from each of $X,Y$ is <identical by descent>. For a noninbred individual, $\phi_{XX}=1/2$, and more generally $\phi_{XX}=(1+F_X)/2$. For distinct individuals with parents $M_X,P_X$, <Mendelian segregation> gives $\phi_{XY}=[\phi_{M_XY}+\phi_{P_XY}]/2$. The offspring's <inbreeding coefficient> equals the parents' kinship coefficient.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Finite-population chromosomal coancestry
{parent=Kinship coefficient}
{title2=$\Phi_{G+1}=\Phi_G+\dfrac{1+F_G-2\Phi_{m,G}}{4(N-1)}$}

For $N$ individuals in monogamous families of exactly two offspring, let $\Phi_G$ be <mean> pairwise <kinship coefficient>, $F_G$ <mean> inbreeding and $\Phi_{m,G}$ <mean> kinship of mates. A random distinct offspring pair are siblings with <probability> $1/(N-1)$. Accounting separately for within-family and cross-family pairs gives the displayed recurrence, while $F_{G+1}=\Phi_{m,G}$. Random mating makes $\Phi_{m,G}=\Phi_G$. Sibling avoidance changes the mating-pair <mean>; it does not create unrelated founders or stop eventual drift to ancestral identity in a closed finite population.

= Sex-linked cousin kinship
{parent=Kinship coefficient}
{title2=$F_X\in\{0,1/8,3/16\}$}

For otherwise outbred opposite-sex <first cousins>, the <probability> that the mother's randomly transmitted X copy matches the father's X by descent depends on which of their parents are siblings. It is zero when the male cousin is related through his father, $1/8$ when the female cousin is related through her father and the male through his mother, and $3/16$ when their mothers are sisters. Daughters can be X-autozygous; sons have one X and a Y and are not homozygous for a paired X chromosome. Recombination and pseudoautosomal regions require a locus-based formulation instead of whole-chromosome blocks.

= Mendelian IBD sharing of full siblings
{parent=Identity by descent}
{c}
{title2=$J\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2,1/2)$}

In an <outbred pedigree>, the two <full siblings> receive the same paternal copy with probability $1/2$, and independently the same maternal copy with probability $1/2$. Thus the number $J$ of shared copies has a <binomial distribution> with probabilities $(1/4,1/2,1/4)$, and $E[J/2]=1/2$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Autozygosity
{parent=Identity by descent}

= Autozygous
{synonym}

<Autozygosity> is <homozygosity> by descent: the maternal and paternal copies in one individual derive from the same ancestral copy. Unlike ordinary <homozygosity>, it concerns the ancestry of chromosome copies rather than only their observed states. In a <pedigree> with one ancestral disease-allele copy and fully penetrant <recessive inheritance>, every affected individual is <autozygous> for that copy.

= Identity by state
{parent=Identity by descent}

= IBS
{synonym}
{c}

Two allele copies are identical by state when their observed states agree, regardless of whether they derive from one recent ancestral copy. Equal marker <haplotypes> can therefore reflect either <identity by descent> or matching founder haplotypes.

= Genetic association
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A genetic association is a statistical dependence between a <genetic variant> and a <phenotype>. An associated marker need not be causal: <linkage disequilibrium> can correlate it with a causal locus, and <population stratification> can produce an association without such a link.

= Genotype relative risk
{parent=Genetic association}
{title2=$R_g=P(D\mid g)/P(D\mid g_0)$}

= Genotypic relative risk
{synonym}

The genotype relative risk compares the <penetrance> for <genotype> $g$ with that of a chosen baseline $g_0$. The common baseline factor cancels when computing <conditional probabilities> of transmitted genotypes given an affected offspring. It is a ratio of disease probabilities, unlike an <odds ratio>.

= Disease-ascertained transmission probability
{parent=Genotype relative risk}
{title2=$P(g\mid g_f,g_m,D)=\frac{q_gR_g}{\sum_hq_hR_h}$}

Let $q_g=P(g\mid g_f,g_m)$ be the <Mendelian segregation> probability, and assume the child's disease status depends on the parental <genotypes> only through its own genotype, with <penetrance> proportional to $R_g$. <Bayes theorem> gives the displayed conditional law. For a diallelic parent pair there are four equally likely labelled transmission outcomes. If an unordered genotype occurs in $m_g$ of them, $q_g=m_g/4$, so its probability is $m_gR_g/\sum_hm_hR_h$. Dropping multiplicities would, for example, misweight a heterozygous child of two heterozygotes.

= Haplotype-relative-risk
{parent=Genetic association}
{wiki}
{title2=$\widehat{\mathrm{OR}}=\frac{wz}{xy}$}

The haplotype-relative-risk design compares transmitted <alleles> to the corresponding untransmitted parental copies, used as internal controls for an affected offspring. If allele-1/allele-2 transmitted counts are $w,x$ and untransmitted counts are $y,z$, the allele-1 <odds ratio> estimate is $wz/(xy)$. An unmatched <Pearson chi-squared test of independence> treats this as a two-by-two table. The <transmission disequilibrium test> instead keeps parent-specific pairing and uses only discordant transmissions. The sampling assumptions of the unmatched calculation and any missing-parent reconstruction must be justified separately.

= Association mapping
{parent=Genetic association}
{wiki}

Association mapping localizes genetic influences on a <phenotype> by testing population <genetic association> with markers. <Linkage disequilibrium> allows a typed marker to tag a nearby causal variant; historical <genetic recombination> can narrow the associated interval. <Population stratification> can produce association without a local causal relation.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Linkage-disequilibrium marker tagging
{parent=Association mapping}

An observed <genetic marker> tags an unobserved causal <genetic variant> when <linkage disequilibrium> correlates their allele indicators. Their squared <allelic correlation> $r^2$ measures prediction strength. Under standard local-alternative score-test approximations, using the marker rather than the causal allele reduces the noncentrality parameter by roughly $r^2$, suggesting a sample-size multiplier about $1/r^2$. This is an approximation requiring the same disease model and sampling design, not a universal power identity.

= Transmission disequilibrium test
{parent=Genetic association}
{wiki}
{title2=$\chi^2_{\mathrm{TDT}}=\frac{(b-c)^2}{b+c}$}

= TDT
{synonym}
{c}

For affected offspring and heterozygous parents at a diallelic locus, let $b$ count transmissions of allele 1 with allele 2 untransmitted and $c$ count the reverse. Under the fair-transmission null, conditional on $b+c$, $b$ is binomial with probability $1/2$. The asymptotic statistic is $(b-c)^2/(b+c)$, with a one-degree-of-freedom chi-squared null distribution. Homozygous-parent transmissions give no contrast. Conditioning within families protects this test from ordinary <population stratification>.

= Incomplete-trio transmission-selection bias
{parent=Transmission disequilibrium test}

If one parent is <heterozygous> and the other parent's <genotype> is missing, a homozygous child reveals the observed parent's transmitted <allele>, whereas a heterozygous child need not. Selecting only revealed transmissions can therefore bias the <transmission disequilibrium test>. For allele-1 frequency $p$ under random mating and fair transmission, the probabilities of an allele-1 or allele-2 homozygous child from an observed heterozygous parent are $p/2$ and $(1-p)/2$. Among selected homozygous children, the observed allele-1 transmission probability is $p$, not generally $1/2$. A complete-trio analysis avoids this reconstruction rule; methods retaining missing-parent families need an explicit <likelihood function> or a valid conditional adjustment. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3297829/[Family-based association tests using genotype data with uncertainty] discusses this bias.

= Family pseudo-control genotype
{parent=Transmission disequilibrium test}

Combine the untransmitted maternal and paternal alleles into a hypothetical control <genotype>, paired with the child's transmitted genotype. Even when two heterozygous parents have a heterozygous child and parent-specific phase is ambiguous, the combined untransmitted genotype is unambiguously heterozygous. Such pseudo-controls are matched family controls, not independent random individuals; <Hardy-Weinberg principle> is therefore not guaranteed merely by this construction.

= Transmitted and untransmitted alleles under multiplicative penetrance
{parent=Family pseudo-control genotype}

Under random mating and <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium>, label each parental allele by whether it is transmitted. Let $(A,B)$ and $(C,D)$ be the transmitted/untransmitted pairs of two parents. Before ascertainment all four draws are independent with population frequencies $\pi$. If offspring disease weights the transmitted pair by $\psi_A\psi_C$, then
$$
\mathbb P(A=a,B=b,C=c,D=d\mid\text{offspring disease})=\pi_a^*\pi_c^*\pi_b\pi_d.
$$
The transmitted pair is an affected-case genotype and the untransmitted pair a population <family pseudo-control genotype> under this homogeneous model. For unordered genotypes, compatible labelled transmissions must be summed. Conditioning on parental genotypes instead removes nuisance population frequencies and gives the usual <transmission disequilibrium test>.

// Target: statistical-modelling.bigb

= Genetic linkage
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Genetic linkage is dependence of transmission at loci on the same chromosome, measured in a two-locus model by a <recombination fraction> below $1/2$. Linkage analysis uses co-segregation within <pedigrees>; <genetic association> instead compares allele or genotype frequencies with phenotype.

= Genetic map
{parent=Genetic linkage}

A genetic map orders <genetic loci> by <genetic linkage> and describes distances inferred from <genetic recombination>. A <recombination fraction> is a probability of a recombinant transmission; map distances count expected exchanges and can be added along a chromosome, whereas recombination fractions generally cannot. Whole-chromosome identity requires the linkage and recombination model, whereas single-locus <kinship coefficients> depend only on descent at that locus.

= Nonparametric linkage analysis
{parent=Genetic linkage}

Nonparametric linkage analysis tests excess <identity by descent> among relatives with a shared <phenotype>, without specifying its complete <penetrance> model or disease-allele frequency. Its null still uses <Mendelian segregation> and the specified <pedigree>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Mean allele-sharing test for affected siblings
{parent=Nonparametric linkage analysis}
{title2=$Z=(\bar J-1)/\sqrt{1/(2n)}$}

For independent pairs of affected <full siblings> with unrelated, noninbred parents and a fully informative <genetic marker>, the no-linkage null gives shared <identity by descent> count $J\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2,1/2)$. Therefore $EJ=1$ and $\operatorname{Var}J=1/2$. An excess-sharing test uses the displayed approximately <standard normal> statistic and an upper-tail critical value. Pairs sharing family members are dependent, so their variance needs separate treatment. This targeted test differs from an omnibus test of all three sharing probabilities.

= Affected-sib-pair analysis
{parent=Nonparametric linkage analysis}

An affected-sib-pair analysis examines marker <identity by descent> between affected <full siblings>. Without <genetic linkage> to the disease locus, the probabilities of sharing zero, one and two copies are $1/4,1/2,1/4$. Excess sharing supplies evidence for linkage under disease models that preferentially transmit a common risk allele to both siblings.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Recessive affected-sib-pair likelihood
{parent=Affected-sib-pair analysis}
{title2=$L_0=u^2,\ L_1=uv,\ L_2=v^2$}

For unaffected <genetic carriers> of a disease with complete <penetrance> and <recessive inheritance>, suppose parental marker-disease <haplotype> phases are equally likely and the marker is fully informative. Write $u=\theta(1-\theta)$ and $v=1/2-u$, where $\theta$ is the <recombination fraction>. Conditional on sharing a parental marker copy, the probability that both siblings inherit that parent's disease allele is $[(1-\theta)^2+\theta^2]/2=v$; conditional on inheriting different marker copies it is $\theta(1-\theta)=u$. Independent paternal and maternal transmissions therefore give $L_0=u^2,L_1=uv,L_2=v^2$. Conditional instead on both siblings being affected, the IBD probabilities are $s^2,2s(1-s),(1-s)^2$, where $s=2u\in[0,1/2]$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Allele-sharing sufficiency for recessive linkage
{parent=Recessive affected-sib-pair likelihood}

For independent affected sib pairs with IBD counts $n_0,n_1,n_2$, the <likelihood function> of the <recombination fraction> is proportional to $s^{2n_0+n_1}(1-s)^{2n_2+n_1}$, with $s=2\theta(1-\theta)$. At fixed sample size, the total shared-copy count $2n_2+n_1$ is a <sufficient statistic> by the <Fisher-Neyman factorization theorem>. Thus a one-sided <likelihood-ratio test> has the same ordering as a test for excess mean IBD sharing. This equivalence depends on the stated fully penetrant recessive model and does not extend to arbitrary disease models.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Parametric linkage analysis
{parent=Genetic linkage}

Parametric linkage analysis forms a <pedigree likelihood> from a specified disease model, including <penetrance>, <allele> frequencies and <recessive inheritance> or another transmission model. It estimates or tests the <recombination fraction> between a disease locus and a <genetic marker>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Complete genetic linkage
{parent=Genetic linkage}
{title2=$\theta=0$}

= Complete linkage
{synonym}

<Complete linkage> in a two-locus model means <recombination fraction> zero: only parental <haplotype> combinations are transmitted. Multiple <genetic markers> in a nonrecombining block represent one <haplotype> segregation observation.

= Homozygosity mapping
{parent=Genetic linkage}

Homozygosity mapping searches for shared homozygous marker regions in individuals with a rare recessive condition, often in an <inbred> pedigree. Under a single ancestral disease copy, affected individuals inherit two copies identical by descent. Marker homozygosity alone gives <identity by state>, so founder haplotype frequencies matter. If a particular haplotype has founder frequency $p$ and $K$ distinct founder chromosome copies underlie all selected descendant copies, their marker-pattern probability is $E[p^K]$. For complete linkage and a single disease copy, the affected-only numerator is $p$, yielding a <LOD score> $\log_{10}(p/E[p^K])$ under linkage-equilibrium founder assumptions.

= LOD score
{parent=Genetic linkage}
{c}
{title2=$Z(\theta)=\log_{10}[L(\theta)/L(1/2)]$}

The LOD score compares the likelihood at recombination fraction $\theta$ with independent assortment:
$$
Z(\theta)=\log_{10}\frac{L(\theta)}{L(1/2)}.
$$
When phenotype likelihood is independent of $\theta$, it can be computed from marker probabilities conditional on phenotype and ascertainment. Positive values support linkage; the observed marker pattern must be specified precisely because affected-only and affected-plus-unaffected observations give different likelihoods.

= Genetic recombination
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Genetic recombination produces a chromosome whose inherited segments come from different parental homologues. In a two-locus meiosis, the <recombination fraction> is the probability that the transmitted combination is recombinant relative to the parental phase.

= Four-gamete test
{parent=Genetic recombination}
{wiki}

Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, two variable sites on one unrecombined rooted tree have nested or disjoint derived-allele clades. At most three of the four biallelic <haplotypes> can then occur. Observation of all four haplotypes proves that the single-tree, single-mutation-per-site model cannot hold: some ancestral <genetic recombination> is necessary if recurrent mutation is excluded. The test gives no proof of absence of recombination when only three haplotypes occur.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Recombination hotspot
{parent=Genetic recombination}
{wiki}

A recombination hotspot is a short region with an elevated local rate of <genetic recombination>. Repeated ancestral breakpoints there can separate neighboring <haplotype blocks> of strong <linkage disequilibrium>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Recombination fraction
{parent=Genetic recombination}
{title2=$0\le\theta\le\tfrac12$}

The recombination fraction $\theta$ is the probability of a recombinant transmission between two loci in an ordinary two-locus linkage model. Complete linkage has $\theta=0$; independent assortment has $\theta=1/2$. Several physical crossovers can restore parental phase, so recombination fraction is not the expected number of crossovers.

= Pedigree
{parent=Genetics}

A pedigree records parent–offspring relationships. A <founder in a pedigree> has no recorded parents; all other individuals have a genotype conditional on their parental genotypes. Inbreeding can produce loops in the undirected diagram while the directed ancestry remains acyclic.

= Outbred pedigree
{parent=Pedigree}

For calculations of <identity by descent>, an outbred pedigree has unrelated, noninbred <founders in a pedigree>, and no additional ancestral relationships beyond those explicitly specified. Founder gene copies are treated as distinct ancestral copies even if they have <identity by state>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= First cousin
{parent=Pedigree}

First cousins are children of <full siblings>. An otherwise outbred first-cousin pair shares two noninbred grandparents and has <kinship coefficient> $1/16$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Full sibling
{parent=Pedigree}

Full siblings share both parents. With unrelated, noninbred <founders in a pedigree>, the two parental contributions give independent opportunities for <identity by descent>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Pedigree likelihood
{parent=Pedigree}

For latent <genotypes> $G_i$ and observed <phenotypes> $X_i$, assume conditional phenotype independence, independent founder genotypes, and conditionally independent Mendelian transmissions. The <likelihood function> is
$$
\sum_{(G_i)}\prod_i P(X_i\mid G_i)\prod_{i\in F}P(G_i)\prod_{i\notin F}P(G_i\mid G_{m(i)},G_{f(i)}).
$$
This follows by the chain rule applied to the acyclic ancestry graph, then the law of total probability over all latent genotypes. <Penetrance> supplies the phenotype factors and <genetic recombination> supplies multilocus transmission factors.

= Phase averaging in a linkage likelihood
{parent=Pedigree likelihood}
{title2=$L(\theta)=\sum_s\pi_sL_s(\theta)$}

An unphased multilocus <genotype> can have several pairs of <haplotypes>. Its <pedigree likelihood> must average over these possible phases with prior weights $\pi_s$, rather than maximize over phases after observing the offspring. Under <linkage disequilibrium>, the weights need not be equal. They can be obtained by conditioning population haplotype frequencies on the observed parental <genotype>; different choices of what is conditioned on change only parameter-independent factors when those frequencies are held fixed.

= Score equation for a phase-averaged linkage likelihood
{parent=Phase averaging in a linkage likelihood}

For a known-phase contribution $L_1(\theta)\propto\theta^{r_1}(1-\theta)^{m_1-r_1}$ and an independent phase-averaged contribution $L_2(\theta)$, an interior maximum satisfies
$$
[r_1-m_1\theta]L_2(\theta)+\theta(1-\theta)L_2'(\theta)=0.
$$
This follows by differentiating $\log L_1+\log L_2$ and multiplying by $\theta(1-\theta)L_2$. The constrained <maximum likelihood estimate> on $[0,1/2]$ must also be compared with the endpoints. An interior root cannot be asserted without the observed pedigree counts; complete nonrecombinant data, for example, have their maximum at zero.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Pedigree graphical model
{parent=Pedigree likelihood}

Assign one <genotype> <random variable> to each person in a <pedigree>. Independent <founders in a pedigree> carry population genotype laws, and each other genotype has its <conditional distribution> given its two parents from <Mendelian segregation>. The ancestry <directed acyclic graph> is a <Bayesian network> for the joint genotype law. Its <moral graph> contains each parent-child edge and each coparent edge. Each founder factor and each child-with-parents factor belongs to a <clique>, giving an undirected graphical representation. The graph is an independence representation; equality with the minimal <conditional independence graph> needs additional faithfulness assumptions, particularly because inheritance can produce zero probabilities.

= Elston-Stewart algorithm
{parent=Pedigree likelihood}
{c}

Pedigree peeling eliminates latent <genotypes> by moving sums inside products whenever the other factors do not depend on the eliminated genotype. For one parent genotype $g$ and one child genotype $h$, the likelihood becomes
$$
\sum_g P(g)P(X_p\mid g)\left[\sum_hP(h\mid g)P(X_c\mid h)\right].
$$
The inner bracket is the child's message to its parent. With two states each, it needs four multiplications and two additions; multiplying the two parent weights and messages needs four more multiplications and the final sum one addition. The unrearranged four terms instead use twelve multiplications. Larger pedigrees use the same variable-elimination identity, with additional handling of pedigree loops.

= Founder in a pedigree
{parent=Pedigree}

= Founders in a pedigree
{synonym}

= Pedigree founder
{synonym}

A founder is an individual whose parents are outside the recorded <pedigree>. The standard <pedigree likelihood> assigns unrelated founders independent genotype distributions based on population allele or haplotype frequencies. If founders are related, their joint prior must instead account for that relationship.

= Recessive inheritance
{parent=Genetics}

In a fully penetrant autosomal recessive model with normal allele $A$ and disease allele $a$, $aa$ is affected and $AA,Aa$ are unaffected. A carrier cross $Aa\times Aa$ has offspring probabilities $1/4,1/2,1/4$ for $AA,Aa,aa$, respectively, from the two independent parental meioses.

= Sibling recurrence risk
{parent=Recessive inheritance}

Sibling recurrence risk is the <conditional probability> that another child has the disease, given an affected sibling. For unaffected parents, complete <penetrance> and a single recessive disease allele, the affected child identifies both parents as <genetic carriers>. Independent <Mendelian segregation> then gives recurrence probability $1/4$, whether or not the parents are related. Incomplete penetrance, phenocopies, different causal loci or affected parents change this calculation.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Genetic carrier
{parent=Recessive inheritance}

A genetic <genetic carrier> has one disease-associated recessive <allele> and one normal <allele> at a <genetic locus>, and is unaffected under a fully penetrant recessive model. Two <genetic carriers> have affected-offspring <probability> $1/4$ under <Mendelian segregation>; a <genetic carrier> and an individual with no disease <allele> have affected-offspring <probability> zero under that same mutation-free model.

= Phenotype
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <phenotype> is an observable characteristic of an <organism>, arising from its <genotype>, environment and other influences. Seed shape is a <phenotype>; $Aa$ is a <genotype>. The distinction prevents appearance from being mistaken for a complete inherited description. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Phenotype][NHGRI: phenotype]{ref}.

For a specified binary <phenotype> $T$, let its <penetrance> in <genotype> $g$ be $r_g=P(T\mid g)$. The <law of total probability> gives $P(T)=\sum_g r_gP(g)$. Thus the ideal $Aa\times Aa$ example has $P(T)=r_{AA}/4+r_{Aa}/2+r_{aa}/4$: the familiar $3/4$ follows only when these <penetrances> are $1,1,0$. A <pedigree likelihood> keeps inheritance and the conditional <phenotype> model as separate factors.

= Dominance
{disambiguate=genetics}
{parent=Phenotype}

= Complete dominance
{parent=Dominance (genetics)}

For a specified characteristic, complete dominance means that the <heterozygote> has the same <phenotype> as one of the two <homozygotes>. It does not mean that the dominant <allele> is more common, more advantageous, or transmitted more often. <Penetrance> specifies the <conditional probability> of an expressed <phenotype> given a <genotype> and is a separate modeling assumption.

= Penetrance
{parent=Phenotype}
{wiki}

Penetrance is the probability of an expressed <phenotype> conditional on a specified <genotype>. It can depend on age, sex and environment. A fully penetrant recessive model assigns disease probability one to the recessive homozygote and zero to other genotypes; incomplete penetrance or phenocopies replace these indicators by intermediate probabilities.

= Genetic variance of penetrance
{parent=Penetrance}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}\{\mathbb E(Y\mid G)\}$}

For a binary disease trait $Y$ with <genotype> $G$, the genetic variance of <penetrance> is the between-genotype term in the <law of total variance>. The averaged within-genotype term is $\mathbb E[q_G(1-q_G)]$. Under <multiplicative diallelic penetrance>, the genetic term is $p^2(\beta^2-\alpha^4)$. This decomposition attributes variance to the modeled locus, not automatically to all hereditary causes.

= Multiplicative diallelic penetrance
{parent=Penetrance}
{title2=$q_G=p\theta^G,\quad G\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2,\phi)$}

With <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium>, let $G$ count copies of the risk <allele>. The <penetrance> $p\theta^G$ must lie in $[0,1]$ for every possible <genotype>. Writing $\alpha=1-\phi+\phi\theta$ and $\beta=1-\phi+\phi\theta^2$ gives $\mathbb E(q_G)=p\alpha^2$ and $\mathbb E(q_G^2)=p^2\beta^2$.

= Genotype
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <genotype> records the inherited <alleles> carried by an individual at one or more <genetic loci>. At a two-copy <diploid> <genetic locus> with <alleles> $A,a$, the possible unordered <genotypes> are $AA,Aa,aa$. The first and last are <homozygotes>; the middle is a <heterozygote>. The notation records inherited states, without yet asserting their <phenotypes>. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/genotype][NHGRI: genotype]{ref}.

An unordered $Aa$ combines the two parental orders $Aa$ and $aA$. This small bookkeeping point explains the factor two in <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> and in an $Aa\times Aa$ cross. If parental origin or arrangement across several <genetic loci> matters, a <phase-known genotype> retains extra information about the two <haplotypes>.

= Genotyping error
{parent=Genotype}

A <genotyping error> is an incorrect observed <allele> or <genotype> call. A probabilistic observation model replaces the deterministic <genetic marker> factor by $P(X=x\mid G=g)$, allowing nonzero <probability> of an observed call inconsistent with the latent <genotype>. Such error can bias family transmission tests and linkage <likelihoods>.

= Heterozygosity
{parent=Genotype}

= Heterozygous
{synonym}

<Heterozygosity> means that the two <allele> states at a diploid <genetic locus> differ; an individual with that property is a <heterozygote>. <Heterozygous> parental <genotypes> provide alternative <alleles> for a <transmission disequilibrium test>.

= Homozygosity
{parent=Genotype}

= Homozygous
{synonym}

<Homozygosity> means that the two <allele> states at a diploid <genetic locus> are the same; an individual with that property is a <homozygote>. Observed <homozygosity> does not by itself establish <identity by descent>.

= Heterozygote
{parent=Genotype}

A heterozygote carries different <alleles> at a diploid locus. Under ordinary Mendelian segregation each is transmitted to an offspring with probability $1/2$. Only heterozygous parents provide a comparison between alternative transmissions in the <transmission disequilibrium test>.

= Homozygote
{parent=Genotype}

A homozygote carries two identical <alleles> at a locus; a <heterozygote> carries different alleles. Homozygosity of an observed <haplotype> is identity by state, and need not be <identity by descent>.

= Haplotype
{parent=Genotype}
{wiki}

A haplotype is the set of <alleles> on one chromosome across specified <genetic loci>. Closely linked markers without intervening <genetic recombination> are transmitted as one haplotype; they are not independent observations of inheritance.

= Haplotype block
{parent=Haplotype}
{wiki}

A haplotype block is a chromosomal region with limited historical <genetic recombination>, strong local <linkage disequilibrium> and comparatively few common <haplotypes>. Its definition is operational and population-dependent; it does not mean that recombination never occurs within it.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Phase-known genotype
{parent=Genotype}

At several <genetic loci>, a phase-known genotype specifies which <alleles> occur together on each chromosome, hence its two <haplotypes>. An unphased multilocus <genotype> may be compatible with several haplotype pairs. For two heterozygous loci, $AB/ab$ and $Ab/aB$ have identical unphased genotypes but different transmission probabilities when <genetic linkage> is present.

= Genetic locus
{parent=Genetics}

= Locus (genetics)
{synonym}

A locus is a position or region of a genome. An <allele> specifies an inherited state there; a <haplotype> specifies states at several loci on one chromosome.

= Allele
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

An <allele> is one of the alternative inherited states at a <genetic locus>, often distinguished by a difference in <DNA> sequence. A population can contain many <alleles>, although a two-copy <diploid> individual carries at most two at that <genetic locus>, one inherited from each parent. See \a[https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Allele][NHGRI: allele]{ref}.

Count copies, not just individuals, when computing <allele frequency>: in 100 individuals with <genotype> counts $49\ AA$, $42\ Aa$, $9\ aa$, there are $2(49)+42=140$ copies of $A$ among 200, so $p_A=0.70$. The <heterozygotes> contribute to both <allele> counts. This converts observed <genotypes> into the population quantity needed by the <Hardy-Weinberg principle>.

= Allele frequency
{parent=Allele}
{title2=$p_A$}

The allele frequency is the proportion of gene copies at a specified <genetic locus> carrying an <allele>. Two unrelated copies can have the same observed allele without being <identical by descent>; under independent sampling their matching probability is the sum of squared allele frequencies. Pedigree <autozygosity> instead traces copies to the same ancestral copy.

= Allele fixation
{parent=Allele frequency}
{title2=$u=0\text{ or }u=1$}

An <allele> fixes when all copies at the relevant locus in the population are that <allele>. Fixation can occur through selection or <genetic drift>. Spatial polymorphism means that fixation within an individual habitat need not imply fixation across a <metapopulation>.

= Population genetics
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Population genetics studies the frequencies of <alleles> and <genotypes> in populations, and their changes through inheritance, mutation, selection, migration and random sampling. <Kingman's coalescent> describes a neutral sample's ancestry backward in time.

= Heterozygote advantage
{parent=Population genetics}

A <heterozygote> has greater <genotype fitness> than either corresponding <homozygote>. In the deterministic random-mating two-allele viability-selection model this gives an attracting interior <allele frequency>, unlike the repelling interior frequency produced by <underdominance>.

= Underdominance
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= Heterozygote disadvantage
{synonym}

<Underdominance> means that the <heterozygote> has lower <genotype fitness> than both corresponding <homozygotes>. In a deterministic randomly mating biallelic population, it produces an unstable interior allele-frequency threshold separating the two fixation outcomes, unlike <heterozygote advantage>.

= Underdominant allele-frequency dynamics
{parent=Underdominance}
{title2=$\dot u=u(1-u)[(a+b)u-b]$}

With <homozygote> fitness advantages $a,b>0$ over the <heterozygote>, the <allele frequency> $u$ of the first <allele> has an unstable threshold $b/(a+b)$. Below it that <allele> decreases; above it it increases. In a discrete viability-selection generation the same numerator gives $u'-u$ after division by <mean> <genotype fitness>. Equal <homozygote> advantages give $\dot u=su(1-u)(2u-1)$, with stable endpoints and unstable midpoint.

= Genotype fitness
{parent=Population genetics}
{title2=$w$}

<Genotype fitness> measures the reproductive or viability success assigned to a <genotype> in a specified selection model. Relative fitnesses weight <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> after viability selection; continuous-time fitness differences instead act as selection-rate differences. The convention and time units must be specified before comparing selection with migration.

= Ancestral recombination graph
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= ARG
{synonym}
{c}

An ancestral recombination graph records ancestral coalescences and <genetic recombination> events for sampled chromosomal material. Backwards in time, each pair of ancestral lineages merges at rate one in standard diploid coalescent units, while a lineage splits at a recombination breakpoint at rate $\rho/2$ times the genetic span of the material it carries. The breakpoint splits that lineage's ancestral material into two parental contributions. Waiting times are exponential with the sum of the current event rates. At each genomic position, retaining the ancestry at that position gives a marginal <phylogenetic tree> with the neutral <Kingman's coalescent> distribution. Different positions have dependent trees because they share parts of the graph.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Population bottleneck
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

A population bottleneck is a temporary large reduction in population size. It changes ancestral coalescence rates and can leave fewer ancestral <haplotypes> and increased <linkage disequilibrium> after the population expands.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Genetic drift
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

Genetic drift is random variation of <allele> frequencies due to finite-population reproduction. In the neutral ancestry description, randomly sampled gene copies merge into common ancestors according to <Kingman's coalescent>.

// Target: probability-and-statistics.bigb

= Effective population size
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$N_e$}

Effective population size is the size of an idealized population giving the relevant rate of <genetic drift> or ancestral coalescence in the population being modeled. In the standard neutral diploid coalescent, time is measured in $2N_e$ generations and the population-scaled <mutation> parameter is $\theta=4N_e\mu$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Segregating site
{parent=Population genetics}
{title2=$S$}

A segregating site is a position at which at least two states occur in the sampled chromosomes. Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, mutations below the sample's common ancestor correspond one-to-one to segregating sites. Their number is commonly denoted $S$.

= Watterson estimator
{parent=Segregating site}
{c}
{title2=$\widehat\theta_W=\frac{S}{\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}1/i}$}

For a neutral sample of size $n\ge2$ under the <infinite sites mutation model>, the Watterson estimator is $\widehat\theta_W=S/a_{n-1}$, where $a_{n-1}=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}1/i$. Since the coalescent has $ES=\theta a_{n-1}$, this estimator is unbiased. It matches the observed segregating-site count to its expected value, but need not maximize the exact <segregating-site likelihood under the neutral coalescent>.

= Asymptotic normality of the Watterson estimator
{parent=Watterson estimator}
{title2=$\sqrt{\log n}(\widehat\theta_W-\theta)\xrightarrow{d}N(0,\theta)$}

For fixed $\theta>0$, a <geometric mutation count in a coalescent epoch> with mean $\mu_i=\theta/i$ has third <moment> $\mu_i+6\mu_i^2+6\mu_i^3$. The centered absolute third moment is at most $4\mathbb E[Y_i^3]+4\mu_i^3$, so its sum is $O(\log n)$. The total <variance> is asymptotic to $\theta\log n$, making the <Lyapunov condition> with $\delta=1$ hold. Rescaling by the <harmonic sum> gives the displayed limit. At $\theta=0$ the estimator is identically zero and the limit is degenerate. The <variance of the Watterson estimator> decreases only logarithmically with sample size at a fixed nonrecombining locus.

= Variance of the Watterson estimator
{parent=Watterson estimator}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta_W)=\frac{\theta}{H_{n-1}}+\frac{\theta^2H_{n-1}^{(2)}}{H_{n-1}^2}$}

The <moments of the mutation count on a neutral coalescent tree> give this <variance> for $\widehat\theta_W=S/H_{n-1}$. Since $H_{n-1}\sim\log n$ and the sum of inverse squares is bounded, it tends to zero. Together with unbiasedness, this gives <mean-square convergence> to the mutation parameter, so it is a <consistent estimator>.

= Infinite sites mutation model
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= Infinitely many sites mutation model
{synonym}

The infinite-sites model assumes that every mutation occurs at a previously unmutated site, so each mutation on the sample genealogy produces a distinct segregating site. If mutation rate per lineage is $\theta/2$ in standard coalescent time units, a tree of total length $L$ has a <Poisson distribution> of mutation count with mean $\theta L/2$.

= Segregating-site likelihood under the neutral coalescent
{parent=Infinite sites mutation model}

For $n\ge2$, the <probability generating function> of the segregating-site count conditional on mutation parameter $\theta$ is
$$
E[z^S\mid\theta]=\prod_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{i}{i+\theta(1-z)}.
$$
This follows by integrating the Poisson generating function over the independent exponential epoch lengths. With $W=L/2$, its density is $(n-1)e^{-w}(1-e^{-w})^{n-2}$. Expanding the binomial factor and integrating $w^k e^{-(\theta+r+1)w}$ gives
$$
P(S=k\mid\theta)=(n-1)\theta^k\sum_{r=0}^{n-2}\frac{(-1)^r\binom{n-2}{r}}{(\theta+r+1)^{k+1}}.
$$
For $k=0$ use $\theta^0=1$, including at $\theta=0$. A posterior density divided by a positive prior density is proportional to this likelihood, allowing posterior simulation output to estimate its maximizer.

= Inbreeding
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= Inbred
{synonym}

Inbreeding is reproduction between genetically related individuals. It increases the chance that an offspring's maternal and paternal copies at a locus are <identical by descent>. Such homozygosity by descent is autozygosity.

= Inbreeding coefficient
{parent=Inbreeding}
{title2=$F$}

The inbreeding coefficient is the <probability> that the two <allele> copies at a locus within an individual are <identical by descent>. It equals the <kinship coefficient> of the individual's parents. It concerns ancestral identity, rather than merely <homozygosity> of observed allelic states.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Inbreeding genotype frequencies
{parent=Inbreeding coefficient}

For a biallelic locus with frequencies $p=1-q$ and $q$, and <inbreeding coefficient> $F$, ancestral-copy identity with probability $F$ gives <genotype> probabilities $P(AA)=p^2+Fpq$, $P(Aa)=2pq(1-F)$ and $P(aa)=q^2+Fpq$. In the IBD component both copies equal a single ancestral draw; in the remaining component the two ancestral alleles are independent population draws. A fully penetrant recessive disease therefore has risk $q^2+Fq(1-q)$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= First-cousin inbreeding coefficient
{parent=Inbreeding coefficient}
{title2=$F=1/16$}

For an otherwise <outbred pedigree>, each of the two shared grandparents contributes $(1/2)^{2+2+1}=1/32$ to the <kinship coefficient> of first cousins. Thus their child has <inbreeding coefficient> $1/16$. Equivalently, cousin kinship is one quarter of the $1/4$ kinship of their sibling parents.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Linkage disequilibrium
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$D=P(AB)-P(A)P(B)$}

= LD
{c}
{synonym}

Linkage disequilibrium is nonindependence of allelic states at different loci in population <haplotypes>. For alleles $A,B$, $D=P(AB)-P(A)P(B)$. It is a population-frequency property, distinct from <genetic linkage>, which concerns transmission within meioses.

= Standardized linkage disequilibrium
{parent=Linkage disequilibrium}
{title2=$D'=D/D_{\max}$}

For positive <linkage disequilibrium> $D$, take $D_{\max}=\min(p_A(1-p_B),(1-p_A)p_B)$; for negative $D$, take $D_{\max}=\min(p_Ap_B,(1-p_A)(1-p_B))$ and retain its negative sign in $D'$. Thus $|D'|\le1$. Its magnitude measures the fraction of the largest disequilibrium possible at the specified allele frequencies. High $|D'|$ need not imply high <allelic correlation>, so it does not alone guarantee good marker prediction.

= Linkage-disequilibrium decay under recombination
{parent=Linkage disequilibrium}
{title2=$D_{t+1}=(1-r)D_t$}

In a random-mating two-locus population with recombination fraction $r$, and no <mutation>, selection, migration or <genetic drift>, the next-generation frequency of haplotype $AB$ is $(1-r)p_{AB}+rp_Ap_B$. The marginal allele frequencies do not change, so subtracting $p_Ap_B$ gives the displayed recursion for <linkage disequilibrium>. Observed disequilibrium also reflects population history and sampling; it is not a deterministic function of physical distance.

= Allelic correlation
{parent=Linkage disequilibrium}
{title2=$r^2=D^2/[p_A(1-p_A)p_B(1-p_B)]$}

For two segregating biallelic loci, encode the allele on a random population <haplotype> by indicator variables $X,Y$. Their <covariance> is the <linkage disequilibrium> coefficient $D$. The squared <correlation> is $r^2=D^2/[p_A(1-p_A)p_B(1-p_B)]$, between zero and one. It measures how well one marker predicts the other and is undefined if either marker is monomorphic.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Population stratification
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Population stratification is systematic variation in allele frequencies between subpopulations. If those subpopulations also differ in outcome causes, <genetic variant> associations can exhibit <confounding>, violating <instrumental-variable independence> in <Mendelian randomization>.

= Hardy-Weinberg principle
{c}
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki=Hardy–Weinberg_principle}
{title2=$p^2,2pq,q^2;\quad p+q=1$}

= Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium
{c}
{synonym}

= HWE
{c}
{synonym}

= Hardy-Weinberg proportions
{c}
{synonym}

The <Hardy-Weinberg principle> describes the <genotype> proportions produced by independently combining two parental <gametes> from the same <allele> pool. For <alleles> $A,a$ with <allele frequencies> $p,q$, where $p+q=1$, the <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> are
$$
P(AA)=p^2,\qquad P(Aa)=2pq,\qquad P(aa)=q^2.
$$
The two ordered <heterozygote> outcomes contribute $pq+qp$. These proportions sum to $(p+q)^2=1$, and their implied $A$ <allele frequency> is $p^2+(2pq)/2=p$. Reshuffling copies therefore preserves their frequencies.

For stable <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium> across generations, use a large population with <random mating>, equal <allele frequencies> in the parental pools, ordinary <Mendelian segregation>, and no <natural selection>, <mutation>, <gene flow> or <genetic drift>. Negligible <genetic drift> is an idealization of large population size. The <one-generation random-mating equilibrium> needs independent union of <gametes>; persistence additionally requires processes that change <allele frequencies> to be absent.

At $p=0.70,q=0.30$, the predicted <genotype> proportions are $0.49,0.42,0.09$. For 100 sampled individuals the <expected values> of the counts are $49,42,9$, rather than the $25,50,25$ of a specified $Aa\times Aa$ cross. The latter fixes both parental <genotypes>; <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> average over population mating.

\Image[hardy-weinberg-proportions.png]
{title=Hardy-Weinberg genotype proportions as allele frequency varies, highlighting p = 0.70}

An observed population need not have these proportions. For example, $70\ AA$, no $Aa$, and $30\ aa$ among 100 individuals still gives $p=0.70$, but has no <heterozygotes>. <Random mating> predicts $0.42$ <heterozygosity> in the next generation under the stated model. A finite sample can also depart by chance, so a discrepancy is a starting point for investigating <inbreeding>, <population stratification> or <genotyping error>, rather than proof of one cause.

Hardy's 1908 note made the conceptual payoff explicit: a dominant <phenotype> does not by itself make its <allele> spread. <Complete dominance> describes expression; changes in frequency require a separate mechanism such as <natural selection>. See \a[https://www-leland.stanford.edu/~huatang/gene244/readings/HardyWeinberg.pdf][Hardy, Mendelian Proportions in a Mixed Population, Science 28 (1908), 49–50]{ref}.

= Multiplicative penetrance preserves Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium
{parent=Hardy-Weinberg principle}
{title2=$\pi_i^*=\pi_i\psi_i/\sum_u\pi_u\psi_u$}

For ordered alleles $I,J$ independently drawn with probabilities $\pi_i$, suppose disease <penetrance> is $c\psi_i\psi_j$. Conditional on disease, the allele draws remain independent with probabilities $\pi_i^*=\pi_i\psi_i/\sum_u\pi_u\psi_u$. This follows by normalizing the product $c\pi_i\pi_j\psi_i\psi_j$. Unordered homozygote probabilities are $(\pi_i^*)^2$ and heterozygote probabilities are $2\pi_i^*\pi_j^*$. Unaffected controls do not generally share this factorization: their genotype weights contain $1-c\psi_i\psi_j$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= One-generation random-mating equilibrium
{parent=Hardy-Weinberg principle}
{title2=$p=u+v/2,\quad(p^2,2p(1-p),(1-p)^2)$}

If parental <genotype> frequencies are $(u,v,w)$ and the two parental gametes are independently sampled from the same <allele> pool, <Mendelian segregation> gives allele frequency $p=u+v/2$. Their independent union has the displayed <Hardy-Weinberg proportions>, irrespective of parental genotype proportions. Equal allele frequencies in the two sexes and no transmission or viability distortion are part of this conclusion.

= Allele-frequency estimator under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium
{parent=Hardy-Weinberg principle}

From genotype counts in $n$ independent diploid individuals, the unbiased and maximum-likelihood estimator of the frequency of allele $B$ is
$$
\widehat\theta=\frac{2n_{BB}+n_{AB}}{2n}.
$$
It is the sample proportion among the $2n$ sampled alleles, so it is consistent and
$$
\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)
\xrightarrow{d}N\left(0,\frac{\theta(1-\theta)}2\right).
$$

= Genetic variant
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A genetic variant is a heritable difference in a DNA sequence relative to a chosen reference. Genetic variants can be used as <instrumental variables> in <Mendelian randomization> when the required causal assumptions are credible.

= Single-nucleotide polymorphism
{parent=Genetic variant}
{wiki}

= SNP
{synonym}
{c}

A single-nucleotide polymorphism is variation at one nucleotide position in sampled genomes. Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, the number of such variable positions in a sample equals the number of mutations on the genealogy below its most recent common ancestor.

= Tag SNP
{parent=Single-nucleotide polymorphism}
{wiki}

A tag SNP represents other nearby <single-nucleotide polymorphisms> whose <alleles> are strongly correlated through <linkage disequilibrium>. Tagging reduces the number of markers needed for <association mapping>; it does not establish that the tag itself causes the phenotype.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Quantitative genetics
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Quantitative genetics studies how genetic and environmental variation contribute to continuously varying traits.

= Genetic risk score
{parent=Quantitative genetics}
{title2=$G_{\rm score}=\sum_j w_jG_j$}

A genetic risk score combines allele counts from several <genetic variants>, usually using specified weights. In <Mendelian randomization> it can act as a combined <instrumental variable> for an <exposure>; interpretation still requires <instrument relevance>, <instrumental-variable independence>, and the <exclusion restriction>.

= Heritability
{title2=$h^2$}
{parent=Quantitative genetics}
{wiki}

Heritability is the fraction of phenotypic variance in a specified population and environment attributed to genetic variation. It is a population parameter, not the genetic fraction of one individual's trait.

= Twin study
{parent=Heritability}
{wiki}

A twin study compares trait resemblance between twins with different degrees of genetic relatedness to separate genetic and environmental variance components.

= Monozygotic twin
{title2=MZ twin}
{parent=Twin study}

This is the single-zygote subtype of a <twin>.

Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote and are modeled as sharing essentially all additive genetic variation.

= Dizygotic twin
{title2=DZ twin}
{parent=Twin study}

This is the two-zygote subtype of a <twin>.

Dizygotic twins develop from two zygotes and, absent assortative mating and other complications, share one half of additive genetic variation on average.

= ACE model
{c}
{parent=Twin study}
{wiki}

The ACE model decomposes trait variation into additive genetic effects $A$, common environmental effects $C$, and unique environmental effects $E$.

= Falconer's formula
{c}
{parent=ACE model}
{wiki}

Falconer's formula estimates narrow-sense heritability from monozygotic and dizygotic twin correlations:
$$
h^2=2(r_{\rm MZ}-r_{\rm DZ}).
$$
It relies on equal relevant environments and a dizygotic additive-genetic correlation of one half.

= Assortative mating
{parent=Quantitative genetics}
{wiki}

Assortative mating is nonrandom mating in which partners are more or less similar than random pairs for a trait or correlated characteristic. Positive assortment can increase genetic similarity among siblings.

= Pathogen
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Medicine
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Infection
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

An <infection> involves invasion and multiplication of an infectious agent in a <host>. <Bacteria> are one possible cause; an agent capable of causing <disease> is a <pathogen>. The National Cancer Institute defines infection through invasion and growth in the body. \a[https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/infection][NCI Dictionary: infection]{ref}.

In a <SIR model>, acquiring <infection> moves a member of the <susceptible compartment> into the <infectious compartment>. That immediate transition is a model assumption: the <SEIR model> instead includes an exposed stage before infectiousness. Specify these states when translating a biological process into <compartmental models (epidemiology)>.

= Immunity
{disambiguate=medical}
{parent=Medicine}

= Immunity
{synonym}

In infection modelling, <immunity> is protection against infection or disease, with its strength and duration represented by the chosen <host> states. A <SIR model> has a permanently protected recovered state, whereas a <SIS model> has no lasting post-recovery protection.

= Isolation
{disambiguate=health care}
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

<Isolation (health care)> separates recognized infected or ill individuals from susceptible contacts to limit transmission. <Quarantine> instead concerns exposed individuals who may or may not be infected.

= Psychoactive drug
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

A psychoactive drug is a substance that changes mental activity, mood or perception. In population <causal inference>, its per-user adverse-event risk and its total population harm are distinct: the latter also depends on how many people use it, for how long, and on substitution between substances.

= Mephedrone
{parent=Psychoactive drug}
{wiki}

Mephedrone is 4-methylmethcathinone, a synthetic stimulant <psychoactive drug>. The chemical identity is described in \a[https://www.euda.europa.eu/publications/drug-profiles/synthetic-cathinones_en][the EUDA synthetic-cathinone profile]. In <causal inference>, an association between increased use and fewer deaths involving another drug does not itself establish substitution: changes in use, population composition and ascertainment must be distinguished.

= MDMA
{c}
{parent=Psychoactive drug}
{wiki}

= Ecstasy
{synonym}

MDMA is 3,4-methylenedioxymethamphetamine, a <psychoactive drug> commonly called ecstasy. A product's street label does not establish its measured chemical contents. Epidemiological comparisons should distinguish single-substance outcomes from outcomes attributed to several drugs.

= Cocaine
{parent=Psychoactive drug}
{wiki}

Cocaine is a stimulant <psychoactive drug>. Comparisons of its population harm require exposure denominators and consistent definitions of drug-related outcomes, rather than death counts alone.

= Informed consent
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

Informed consent is voluntary agreement after receiving and understanding relevant information about an intervention or participation in research. In a <clinical trial>, the participant is informed about the study procedures, potential benefits and risks, alternatives and the option to decline or withdraw. Consent precedes <randomization>.

= Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= HSCT
{synonym}
{c}

= Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation
{synonym}

= Neurology
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= Neuroimaging
{parent=Neurology}
{wiki}

= Magnetoencephalography
{parent=Neuroimaging}
{title2=MEG}
{wiki}

= Electroencephalography
{parent=Neuroimaging}
{title2=EEG}
{wiki}

= Migraine
{parent=Neurology}
{wiki}

Migraine is a neurological disorder that can be an outcome in a <clinical trial> or an <observational study>. Its statistical association with an <exposure> does not alone establish a <causal effect>.

= Disease
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= Prion disease
{parent=Disease}

A prion disease is associated with self-propagating abnormal folding of a <protein>. Its epidemiological interpretation requires distinguishing exposure, infection, clinical onset and diagnostic detection.

= Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease
{parent=Prion disease}

= vCJD
{c}
{synonym}

A human <prion disease> associated with exposure to the <BSE> agent. Exposure totals and clinical-onset totals measure different stages of the disease process; <incubation periods> and ascertainment affect their relationship.

= Scrapie
{parent=Prion disease}

A <prion disease> of sheep and goats. A <scrapie> test result and a <BSE> diagnosis are distinct outcomes, even when the clinical appearances overlap.

= Bovine spongiform encephalopathy
{parent=Prion disease}

= BSE
{c}
{synonym}

A <prion disease> of cattle. A positive surveillance test is an observed diagnostic outcome, whose frequency need not equal true infection <prevalence> unless test performance and sampling are accounted for.

= Myelodysplastic syndrome
{parent=Disease}
{wiki}

= MDS
{synonym}
{c}

= Chronic disease
{parent=Disease}

A chronic disease persists over an extended period. A <continuous-time multi-state model> can represent its progression through severity states, but long duration alone does not imply irreversible progression or justify ignoring state misclassification.
